\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  WEEK AHEAD:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Action Could Weigh On Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2012\par 03:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Central banks could respond to weak global economic outlook\par \par \uc2 --ECB, RBA could cut rates\par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent yen from strengthening\par \par \uc2 By Anusha Shrivastava\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--The dollar could face a rocky road next week as central banks respond to a darkening economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 Nonfarm payrolls in the U.S. grew by a lackluster 69,000 last month, the Labor Department said Friday, the smallest gain in a year. The unemployment rate was 8.2%.\par \par \uc2 This poor showing has raised expectations for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to embark on another bond-buying program or other measures to boost the economy. Such moves in the past weakened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by increasing the supply of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Between the worsening economic outlook and the lack of resolution for the European debt crisis, "there will be an effective policy response," said Jason Shell, head of North American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 Deutsche Bank AG\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke is scheduled to testify before a congressional panel on the economic outlook, and market participants are waiting for any hints of further easing. The Fed's policy-making committee next meets June 19-20.\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also will mull the worsening economic landscape when it updates markets on policy Wednesday. A rate decision from the ECB will be followed that day by a press conference with ECB President Mario Draghi. Some market participants are expecting a 25-basis-point cut in rates to 0.75% from 1%.\par \par \uc2 "If the ECB eases, we may get a bounce in the euro," said Bob Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at RBS in Stamford, Conn. The market has been short euros and the move is overextended, he said. A policy response from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would indicate it is working toward resolving the situation and boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sinche, like other traders and market participants, thinks the likelihood of the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week to prevent further strengthening of the yen is high. The yen, which is a traditional safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has been gaining versus the euro and the dollar as investors seek nonrisky assets.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Japanese government went on high alert against the newly rising yen as it rose versus the dollar. Word spread in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  was calling financial institutions to ask the latest dollar/yen price, a move often seen as a sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering buying dollars on behalf of the government to prop up the price of the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Calls for Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates by 50 basis points are also growing amid doubts on China's economic outlook and stalling of the local economy. China is Australia's biggest trading partner. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 , or the RBA, already has cut rates by 100 basis points since November. Its next meeting is scheduled for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England also will hold its June meeting next week to decide on interest rates and quantitative easing or further bond-buying. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably won't change its benchmark interest rate of 0.5%, according to a Dow Jones Newswires survey of economists, but the Monetary Policy Committee may be increasingly swayed toward boosting the bond-buying program if the May purchasing managers' index for the services sector, due to be published next week, shows as big a drop as the manufacturing sector's PMI for the same month.\par \par \uc2 "If the BoE jumps in, sterling would weaken," said Alan Ruskin at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Overall though, he thinks the central banks may want to "keep their powder dry" because they would want to wait until after the Greek elections on June 17.\par \par \uc2 If the pro-austerity group in Greece wins, the country may manage to stay within the euro zone as it receives the next tranche of its bailout funds. If not, the likelihood of it leaving the euro would increase, creating further uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 -By Anusha Shrivastava, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2227; anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com; @djfxtrader\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120602e8620002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
