\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: Japan Warns Against Rising Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kelly Olsen   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2012\par 02:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Japanese government went on high alert against the newly rising yen Friday, trying to scare off global investors with multiple threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but stopping short of direct action to drive the yen down.\par \par \uc2 A number of fresh signs of economic weakness from around the world over the past week have boosted the yen against the dollar and the euro, as investors have poured money into what is considered one of the globe's few remaining safe-haven assets.\par \par \uc2 After a surprisingly weak U.S. jobs report Friday morning pushed the dollar below Y78 for the first time in months, word spread in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  was calling financial institutions to ask the latest dollar/yen price, a move often seen as a sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was mulling buying dollars on behalf of the government to prop up the price of the greenback.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 While traders say no purchases took place, the reports pushed the dollar back up above the Y78 level.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials and business executives have complained that the yen's rise against the dollar and euro is raising the price of Japanese-made goods in world markets, crimping exports and threatening to undermine a tentative economic recovery from last year's natural disasters.\par \par \uc2 After the yen rose steadily in value through the week, a raft of officials came out during the Tokyo day Friday to issue blunt, public threats that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in five months.\par \par \uc2 "If these excessive moves continue, I will have to respond decisively," Finance Minister Jun Azumi said at a regular news conference, using language that suggests the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sharply increased. "I'm saying I will take decisive measures and I mean it."\par \par \uc2 The comments were far tougher than those made by the minister on Thursday, when Mr. Azumi didn't overtly threaten to act to push the yen lower and said only that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves were speculative and out of line with Japan's economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Mr. Azumi said speculators are taking advantage of financial uncertainty in Europe to "conspicuously" drive up the value of the yen.\par \par \uc2 "It's clear that one-sided yen strength does not reflect the real state of Japan's economy," he said. "The G-7 and others have repeatedly confirmed that excessive movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are undesirable."\par \par \uc2 Investors are seeking refuge in the yen amid turmoil in Europe over the region's debt crisis. Worries over financial woes in Greece and increasing concerns about Spain have roiled global financial markets and sent risk assets such as stocks sharply lower.\par \par \uc2 In late Friday trading in New York, the dollar was at about Y78.08. That compares with its all-time low of Y75.31 reached Oct. 31, which triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largest-ever single-day \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities sold Y8.072 trillion on Oct. 31 and Y1.019 trillion in the four days afterward.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials, including Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda, have said in recent weeks that Tokyo wouldn't be bound by the views of other nations if it decided to take action, shrugging off speculation that U.S. criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made such moves difficult.\par \par \uc2 Vice Finance Minister Takehiko Nakao, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat, specifically raised concerns over the yen's rise against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 If "excessive moves continue not just against the euro, but also the dollar, we'll respond decisively," he said at a conference in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Other Japanese officials echoed Mr. Azumi's comments, with Chief Cabinet Secretary Osamu Fujimura saying that Tokyo must take "decisive action" if the yen's "excessive" appreciation continues.\par \par \uc2 Motohisa Furukawa, Japan's economy minister, expressed his concerns over the impact of the stronger yen on the country's fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "If this risk-aversion in the market [further] affects the stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, that could worsen the sentiment among consumers and corporations that had been improving and put downward pressure on the real economy," he told reporters.\par \par \uc2 --Kosaku Narioka and Toko Sekiguchi contributed to this article. [ 06-01-12 2011ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120602e86200003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
