\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 2,145 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2012\par 01:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    78.86-79.29      78.61-79.64 \par EUR/USD   1.2351-1.2475    1.2151-1.2505 \par AUD/USD   0.9686-0.9803    0.9659-0.9856 \par NZD/USD   0.7509-0.7620    0.7477-0.7647 \par GBP/USD   1.5276-1.5614    1.5233-1.5647 \par USD/CHF   0.9627-0.9785    0.9596-1.0000 \par USD/CAD   1.0269-1.0318    1.0240-1.0423 \par EUR/JPY    97.00-98.70      96.00-99.42 \par EUR/GBP   0.7968-0.8005    0.7947-0.8045 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting three-month low of 78.86 Wednesday. Undermined by flows to safe-haven JPY and unwinding of JPY-funded carry trades amid increased risk aversion (VIX fear gauge surged 14.79% to 24.14, DJIA fell 1.28%) as worries about Spain's banking problems escalated, while concerns stoked over pace of U.S. economic recovery after surprise 5.5% on-month fall in U.S. April pending home sales index to 95.5 (vs forecast for no change). USD/JPY also weighed by lower U.S. Treasury yields; Japan exporter sales; Xinhua news agency report quashing hopes of large-scale stimulus steps from Beijing to boost slowing Chinese economy. But USD/JPY losses also tempered by demand from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importers; expectations of stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Data focus: 2350 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  April preliminary industrial production, 0500 GMT Japan April housing starts & construction orders, 1200 GMT Fed's Pianalto speech, 1215 GMT U.S. May ADP national employment report (jobs expected +150,000), 1230 GMT U.S. 1Q GDP (2nd estimate -- seen revised down to 1.9% from 2.2%), 1230 GMT U.S. May 26 weekly jobless claims, 1345 GMT U.S. May ISM-Chicago PMI (expected 56.0). USD/JPY daily chart negative-biased as 5- & 15-day moving averages falling; MACD bearish, stochastics revert to bearish mode. Support at 78.86 (Wednesday's low), then at 78.61 (200-day moving average); breach would expose downside to 77.36 (Feb. 14 low), then 76.49 (Feb. 6 low). Resistance at 79.29 (hourly chart) then at 79.57-79.64 band (Wednesday's high-Tuesday's high) and 79.73-79.82 band (Monday's high-Friday's high); breach would target 80.08-80.14 band (May 23 high-May 22 high), then 80.39 (May 17 high), 80.56-80.61 band (May 16 high-May 2 high) and 80.95 (55-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting near-23-month low of 1.2360 Wednesday. Undermined by Europe's lack of coordinated policy response to tackle region's debt crisis; mounting anxiety over Span's banking system as European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rejects Madrid's plan to indirectly tap cheap ECB lending facilities to recapitalize Bankia; increased fears about Spain's fiscal health as country's 10-year government yields Wednesday rose to 6.65%, approaching levels that precipitated international bailouts for Greece, Portugal and Ireland; fears of debt contagion to Italy as Rome fell short of selling the maximum targeted amount of debt in a pair of bond auctions Wednesday that attracted only lukewarm demand; increased fears of Greece's exit from the euro after latest polls showed Greek conservatives losing lead to leftists ahead of June 17 election; \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrade of nine Danish financial institutions; worries about euro-zone economic slowdown as region's economic sentiment indicator fell more than expected to 90.6 in May from 92.9 in April (vs 92.0 forecast). But euro sentiment soothed by EU Commission saying countries that use the euro should set up a "banking union" that allows them to share the burden of bank failures. Data focus: 0600 GMT Germany April retail sales, 0800 GMT Germany May job data, 0900 GMT May flash estimate euro area inflation. EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode, although latter at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 1.2351 (base of descending channel formed with May 1 high of 1.3283 and May 16 low of 1.2679); breach would expose downside to 1.2151 (June 29, 2010 low), then the psychological 1.2000 level. Resistance at 1.2475 (hourly chart), then at 1.2505 (Wednesday's high) and 1.2575 (Tuesday's high); breach would target 1.2597 (top of channel), then 1.2625 (Monday's high), 1.2825 (May 21 high) and 1.2869 (May 15 high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting six-day low of 0.9693 this morning. Undermined by euro-zone worries; disappointing Australia April retail sales; expectations of further rate cuts from RBA in coming months; weaker commodity prices (CRB spot index closed down 4.69 Wednesday at 275.05); month-end portfolio hedge adjustments; Aussie sales on soft AUD/NZD cross. But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie. Data focus: 0130 GMT Australia April financial aggregates (including private sector credit), April building approvals, 1Q private new capital expenditure & expected expenditure, 0630 GMT Australia April international reserves. Daily chart negative-biased as 5- & 15-day moving averages falling; MACD & stochastics turned bearish. Support at 0.9686 (May 23 six-month low), then at 0.9659 (Nov. 23 swing low); breach would expose downside to 0.9386 (Oct. 4 swing low). Resistance at 0.9803 (hourly chart), then at 0.9856 (hourly chart) and 0.9897 (Tuesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9934 (May 22 high), then 0.9963-0.9968 band (May 17 high-May 16 high) and 1.0016 (May 15 high).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting four-day low of 0.7512 this morning. Undermined by euro-zone worries; softer commodity prices. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap; Kiwi demand on softer AUD/NZD cross. Data focus: 0100 GMT New Zealand May NBNZ business outlook. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics turned bearish. Support at 0.7509 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7477 (May 24 low), then 0.7453 (May 23 near-six-month low), 0.7367 (Nov. 25 swing low) and 0.7113 (March 17, 2011 swing low). Resistance at 0.7620 (Wednesday's high), then at 0.7647 (Tuesday's high); breach would target 0.7675 (May 22 high), then 0.7696 (May 16 high), 0.7797 (May 15 high) and 0.7824 (May 14 high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting four-month low of 1.5470 this morning. Undermined by euro-zone worries; European Commission survey showing the overall measure of business and consumer confidence in the U.K. fell by 4.7 points to 91.0 in May from 95.7 in April, a faster rate than in any other European country in May; possibility of further quantitative easing from Bank of England as euro zone's problems deepen. But GBP/USD losses tempered by GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Data focus: 0600 GMT UK May Nationwide house price index. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. There is no significant support until 1.5276 (Jan. 16 low), then at 1.5233 (Jan. 12 swing low); breach would expose downside to psychological 1.5000 line, then 1.4946 (July 12, 2010 low). Resistance at 1.5614 (hourly chart), then at 1.5647 (hourly chart) and 1.5716-1.5726 band (Tuesday's high-May 24 high); breach would expose upside to 1.5848 (May 22 high), then 1.5932 (May 17 high), 1.6113-1.6122 band (May 15 high-May 14 high) and 1.6142 (May 11 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting 15-month high of 0.9717 this morning. Underpinned by broadly stronger demand for safe-haven USD as risk aversion rises over Spain's woes; \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as EUR/CHF hovers just above 1.2000 floor (cross last at 1.2006). But CHF sentiment soothed by higher-than-expected rise in Swiss KOF leading economic indicator to 0.81 in May from a revised 0.43 in April (vs 0.52 forecast). USD/CHF gains also tempered by CHF demand on EUR/CHF cross as euro-zone fears persist. Data focus: 0545 GMT Swiss 1Q GDP. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode, although latter at overbought; 5- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at 0.9775-0.9785 band (Feb. 11, 2011 high-Jan. 11, 2011 high); breach would expose upside to psychological 1.0000 line, then 1.0066 (Dec. 1, 2010 high. Support at 0.9627 (hourly chart), then at 0.9596 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 0.9555 (Tuesday's low), then 0.9527 (Monday's low), 0.9462 (May 23 low) and 0.9363 (May 21 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade with bullish bias. Underpinned by euro-zone worries; weaker commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled down $2.94 Wednesday at $87.82/bbl); flat Canadian April producer prices (vs forecast for +0.1% on month rise). Data focus: 1230 GMT Canada 1Q balance of payments. USD/CAD daily chart positive-biased as 5- & 15-day moving averages rising; MACD & stochastics turned bullish. Resistance at 1.0312-1.0318 band (Friday's four-month high-Jan. 9 high); breach would expose upside to 1.0414-1.0423 band (Dec. 19 high-Dec. 14 high). Support at 1.0269 (hourly chart), then at 1.0240 (hourly chart), 1.0218 (Wednesday's low) and 1.0204 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0149 (May 22 low), then 1.0135 (May 18 low), 1.0101 (May 17 low) and 1.0051 (May 16 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting four-month low of 97.74 Wednesday. Undermined by worries over Spain; Japan exporter sales. But EUR/JPY losses tempered by Japan importer demand. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 97.04-97.00 (Jan. 16 swing low- psychological line); breach would expose downside to psychological round-numbered levels of 96.00 down to 92.00, then 91.35 (November 2000 low). Resistance at 98.70 (hourly chart), then at 99.42 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 99.94 (Tuesday's high), then 100.26-100.33 band (Monday's high-Friday's high), 101.48 (May 23 high) and 102.13 (May 22 high). [ 30-05-12 2332GMT ]\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to consolidate after hitting two-week low of 0.7968 Wednesday. Daily chart mixed as MACD in bullish mode; but stochastics bearish; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Resistance at 0.7999-0.8005 (Wednesday's high-Tuesday's high), then at 0.8036-0.8045 band (Monday's high-Friday's high) and 0.8061 (May 23 high); breach would expose upside to 0.8102 (May 22 high), then 0.8119 (previous base set April 30), 0.8138-0.8142 (May 4 high-May 3 high) and 0.8162 (May 2 high). Support at 0.7968 (Wednesday's low), then at 0.7947 (May 16 three-year low); breach would expose downside to 0.7807 (Oct. 31, 2008 minor reaction low), then 0.7692 (Oct. 20, 2008 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 -By Jerry Tan, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4046; Jerry.tan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might con