\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-BoE's Posen says govt role needed to clean up euro zone banks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rie Ishiguro   \par \uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2012\par 10:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 21 (Reuters) - Bank of England policymaker Adam Posen said on Monday that the euro zone crisis calls for governments' active role to recapitalise and clean up the region's banking system, saying monetary policy alone won't solve structural problems.\par \par \uc2 "The biggest thing you need is forcefully injecting enough capital into the banking system," Posen said at a Tokyo conference on global financial regulation hosted by the Columbia Business School.\par \par \uc2 "The source of current problems is not Greece... The source of current problems in the euro zone is that various financial exposures we all have in the interbank market are not yet resolved because certain financial institutions are insufficiently capitalised, insufficiently disciplined," he said.\par \par \uc2 The failure in Athens to form a government has stoked fears of a disorderly Greek exit from the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could trigger meltdown of the global banking system.\par \par \uc2 Investors are expected to stay wary at least until the June 17 Greek election makes clear whether the nation will stay or leave the euro.\par \par \uc2 Posen said that a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions taken around the world to contain the crisis, including the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's three-year funding, have prevented things from getting worse. But monetary policy alone won't solve structural problems such as those of the banking system.\par \par \uc2 "Good monetary policy will not solve structural problems, bad monetary policy will make all structural problems insoluble... Institutions require very active government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  made headway in cleaning up its banking system after a crisis in the 1990s and the United States and Britain have partially resolved recent woes, "it is a path far from under way in the euro area," he said, adding that countries need not be afraid to nationalise banks.\par \par \uc2 Last week, European Union finance ministers agreed on tougher capital rules for banks, resolving years of sparring between Britain and the rest of the EU over how to craft measures to prevent another financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Britain has been urging its banks to make contingency plans for at least six months, and the BoE has said that irrespective of whether there was a euro zone break-up, the way ahead would be painful for Britain as well as the rest of the EU.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Michael Watson and Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 BRITAIN BOE/POSEN  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | J | M | NAT | U | UKI | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120521e85l000fn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
