\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Former Fin Min Fujii: Govt Must  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  if Yen Rises Sharply\b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2012\par 12:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Former Japanese finance minister Fujii says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  imperative if yen surges\par \par \uc2 --Says yen's current levels are out of line with economic fundamentals\par \par \uc2 --Notes monetary easing can't change yen trend but could temporarily curb its strength\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A Japanese ruling party heavyweight said Wednesday the government must \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets if the yen rises sharply, adding to signs that the country is standing ready to protect its economy from global financial turmoil that has driven the yen higher in recent weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It will be imperative for (the government) to take steps such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," if the yen surges to threaten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's export-reliant economy, Hirohisa Fujii, a former finance minister and current tax policy chief of the ruling Democratic Party of Japan, said in an interview.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I believe that the yen's current high levels are out of line with the real economy," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fujii, who is seen as being a close adviser to Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda on economic and financial issues, said that gaining approval from other nations, including the U.S., on any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen would be "impossible," partly because such a move would benefit Japanese exporters at the expense of their overseas rivals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even so, "we must" do it if necessary, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen has broadly strengthened over recent weeks as global concerns over a rekindled euro-zone debt crisis have driven up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which is considered a relatively safe investment. As of 1045 GMT, the yen was at Y80.39 against the dollar and at Y102.22 against the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fujii's remarks suggest that Japanese officials are determined not to let a resurgent yen obstruct the country's nascent economic recovery and undermine their main priority for fiscal reform: the doubling of the country's sales tax.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although few investors expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  anytime soon, officials in Tokyo have hardened their rhetoric over recent weeks to try to discourage speculators from buying the yen, with Prime Minister Noda signaling his openness to unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a recent interview with The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other Japanese officials have told Dow Jones Newswires that the strong yen and deflation need to be tackled to ensure that lawmakers support the passage of the tax bills this year, adding that it is "inconceivable" that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would hold off on any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because of a planned U.S. presidential election in November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fujii, who during his time as finance minister expressed frequent objections to the idea of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, stressed that such a move would only be a "a temporary measure" and could not reverse market trends.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fujii said monetary easing by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  "can never be a driver of real economic growth" or change the yen's trend, but he added that it could temporarily weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is one option, and... pushing interest rates lower through monetary policy could also contribute," to bringing down the yen, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The comments on using monetary policy to bring down the yen--even temporarily--add to political pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and come even though Fujii is typically one of the few ruling party lawmakers who remains sympathetic to the BOJ.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ has eased its monetary policy on several occasions in recent months in the face of intense political pressure to do more to defeat deflation and boost growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 16-05-12 1059GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120516e85g000rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
