\par\par \b \uc2 DJ INTERVIEW:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  PM Noda Doesn't Rule Out Solo  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2012\par 04:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japanese Prime Minister Noda doesn't rule out Tokyo taking unilateral action to stem yen's rise\par \par \uc2 -- Noda stops short of saying whether \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now over-valued\par \par \uc2 -- Noda calls on BOJ to fight not just deflation, but also strong yen\par \par \uc2 -- Says European debt crisis biggest risk factor for Japan economy\par \par \uc2 (Editor's Note: This story first ran on Saturday.)\par \par \uc2 By George Nishiyama and Yuka Hayashi\par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda on Saturday did not rule out Tokyo taking unilateral action in the future to stem the yen's rise, but stopped short of saying whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was now over-valued.\par \par \uc2 Noda singled out the European debt crisis--a factor behind the yen's strength--as the biggest risk for the Japanese economy, and called on the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to fight not just deflation, but also the strong yen.\par \par \uc2 Asked about prevailing views in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen's rise without the support of the U.S., Noda said in an interview that all options were open.\par \par \uc2 "There were times when we received cooperation," he said, referring to the joint yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Group of Seven nations in March of last year after Japan was hit by a magnitude-9.0 earthquake.\par \par \uc2 "But there were times when we acted alone. There can be various approaches."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  subsequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own on several times last year to reverse the yen's rise, but its actions were criticized by the U.S. Treasury in December, leading market participants to believe that Tokyo would now stay put.\par \par \uc2 While a credit easing by the BOJ in February lifted the dollar above Y84, the dollar has fallen back below Y80 recently due to renewed concerns over the European debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Noda, who next week will attend a Group of Eight summit, said he hoped to discuss with the leaders ways to contain the crisis, noting that Japan had made its effort through a $60 billion pledge to the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 While Europe must first take measures on its own to prevent contagion, Noda said other nations cannot afford to sit idle.\par \par \uc2 "It is risky to just stand on the sidelines and watch. This is not just about Europe, there is a possibility that it may spread to the global economy," he said. "Of course this is the biggest downside risk factor for the Japanese economy."\par \par \uc2 The prime minister said Japanese authorities were keeping a close eye on the yen's recent rise.\par \par \uc2 "We need to closely monitor the market to see whether the recent moves deviate from the real state of the economy or are driven by speculative moves."\par \par \uc2 Noda also turned to the BOJ to help battle not just deflation, but also the persistent strength of the yen.\par \par \uc2 "We share with the BOJ the view that the strong yen and deflation are challenges for the economy that must be overcome," Noda said, adding: "I hope the BOJ will take bold steps in a timely and appropriate manner with such thinking in mind."\par \par \uc2 In a move seen as a response to political pressure, the BOJ decided in February to buy more government bonds and make a clear commitment to seek a 1% inflation goal, taking the markets by surprise and reversing the yen's climb.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also moved to buy more bonds in late April, but the action was seen as within expectations and failed to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Noda, who later on Saturday headed to Beijing for a summit with his Chinese and South Korean counterparts, pledged to speed up efforts to form a free trade agreement among Asia's three biggest economies.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese leader said Tokyo wants to start formal negotiations on the trilateral pact "as soon as possible," and will urge the other two nations to join in the deal, which could create an integrated trade zone rivaling in size the North American Free Trade Agreement and the European Union.\par \par \uc2 Japan has lagged behind rivals, particularly South Korea, in sealing free trade pacts in recent years, much to the anguish of corporate Japan, which feels that it is at a disadvantage against Korean rivals in markets such as the EU with which Seoul already has a deal.\par \par \uc2 In Beijing, the focus will also be on how Noda handles territorial issues between Japan and China, including the Senkaku islands in the East China Sea.\par \par \uc2 The islands, known as Daioyu in China, are in the spotlight after Tokyo's maverick governor, Shintaro Ishihara, announced plans for the metropolitan government to purchase three of the islands.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether the national government may buy the privately-held islands instead, Noda stressed that the territory was now under Japan's "valid control," adding that he would mull all options to ensure that in the future.\par \par \uc2 -By George Nishiyama and Yuka Hayashi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; george.nishiyama@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120514e85e001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip