\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 2,090 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2012\par 01:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    79.42-79.94      79.14-80.08 \par EUR/USD   1.2911-1.2972    1.2854-1.3008 \par AUD/USD   1.0017-1.0085    1.0000-1.0116 \par NZD/USD   0.7809-0.7866    0.7767-0.7883 \par GBP/USD   1.6067-1.6158    1.6035-1.6201 \par USD/CHF   0.9256-0.9301    0.9230-0.9340 \par USD/CAD   0.9998-1.0070    0.9974-1.0147 \par EUR/JPY   102.76-103.41    102.54-103.73 \par EUR/GBP   0.8012-0.8060    0.8000-0.8072 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting near-three-month low of 79.42 yesterday. Undermined by flows to safe-haven JPY and unwinding of JPY-funded carry trades amid increased risk aversion (VIX fear gauge rose 5.41% to 20.08, DJIA fell sixth straight day to close down 0.75%) as fears mount over political uncertainty in Greece and Spain's banking system. USD/JPY also weighed by Japan exporter sales. But USD/JPY losses tempered by demand from Japan importers and investment trusts; expectations of stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yen crosses vulnerable to China April trade balance data (likely to post larger trade surplus of $10.4 billion in April compared with March's $5.35 billion surplus, but year-on-year export growth likely slowed to +8.5% versus +8.9% in March; imports are seen up 10% versus March's 5.3% increase, according to a poll of 14 economists conducted by Dow Jones Newswires). Data focus: 2350 GMT Japan March balance of payments, April provisional trade statistics for 1st 20 days, April bank lending, April international transactions in securities, 0500 GMT Japan April economy watchers survey, 1230 GMT U.S. March trade balance, May 5 weekly jobless claims, April import & export price indexes, 1500 GMT Fed's Kocherlakota speech; Fed's Ben Bernanke and Charles Evans speeches at Chicago Fed conference. USD/JPY daily chart negative-biased as 5- & 15-day moving averages falling; MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold. Support at 79.42 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 79.14 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of advance from Feb. 1 low of 76.02 to March 15 high of 84.18), then 78.41 (200-day moving average). Resistance at 79.94 (yesterday's high), then at 80.08 (Tuesday's high), 80.40 (Friday's high) and 80.55-80.61 band (May 3 high-May 2 high); breach would expose upside to 81.45 (April 27 high), then 81.71 (April 25 high), 81.78-81.87 band April 20 high-April 10 high) and 82.56 (April 6 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade with bearish bias after hitting near-four-month low of 1.2911 yesterday. Undermined by fears that political impasse in Greece could unravel recent EU/IMF bailout deal or result in the country leaving the euro bloc; concerns over Spain's fiscal health as 10-year government bond yields jumped above 6% yesterday and additional banking provisions totaling more than EUR30 billion against property loans expected to be announced by the government tomorrow could result in weaker banks seeking additional capital from the state; meanwhile, Spain yesterday took a 45% part-nationalization stake in Spain third largest bank Bankia. EUR/USD also weighed by worries about economic slowdown in the euro zone. But euro sentiment soothed after euro-zone governments said they would release EUR4.2 billion in previously agreed financing for Greece; rise in German exports and imports in March to record highs which eased concern Germany could fall into recession. EUR/USD losses also tempered by sovereign demand for euro. Data focus: 1000 GMT 2Q German Ifo economic climate for the euro area. EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 1.2911 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside 1.2854 (Jan. 23 low), then 1.2624 (Jan. 13 reaction low). Resistance at 1.2972 (hourly chart), then at 1.3008 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.3067 (Tuesday's high), then 1.3116 (100-day moving average), 1.3179-1.3180 (Friday's high-May 3 high) and 1.3242 (May 2 high, near downtrend resistance line from Feb. 29 high of 1.3486).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate with negative bias after hitting near-five-month low of 1.0017 yesterday. Spotlight on 0130 GMT Australia April labour force data. AUD/USD undermined by fears over Greece and Spain; softer commodity prices (CRB spot index closed down 0.3 yesterday at 294.83); expectations of further rate cuts from RBA in coming months. But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie. Aussie vulnerable to China April trade balance data. Daily chart negative-biased as 5- & 15-day moving averages falling; MACD bearish; stochastics stays suppressed at oversold. Support at 1.0017 (yesterday's low), then at psychological 1.0000 line (near base of descending channel formed with Feb. 29 high of 1.0856 and April 4 low of 1.0238); breach would expose downside to 0.9857 (Dec. 15 reaction low). Resistance at 1.0085 (hourly chart), then at 1.0116 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0218-1.0221 (Tuesday's high-previous base set April 11), then 1.0278 (Friday's high), 1.0354 (May 2 high) and 1.0402 (top of channel).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to consolidate with negative bias after hitting four-month low of 0.7809 yesterday. Undermined by worries about Greece and Spain; softer commodity prices. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap. Daily chart negative-biased as 5- & 15-day moving averages falling; MACD bearish; stochastics stays suppressed at oversold. Support at 0.7809 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7767 (Jan. 9 low), then 0.7648 (Dec. 29 minor reaction low). Resistance at 0.7866 (hourly chart), then at 0.7883 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.7973 (Tuesday's high), then 0.8019 (Friday's high), 0.8153 (May 2 high) and 0.8189 (May 1 high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with negative bias after hitting two-week low of 1.6067 yesterday as markets await 1100 GMT BOE interest rate decision: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to leave its policy interest rate unchanged at 0.50% and hold its bond-buying total at GBP325 billion. GBP/USD undermined by worries about Greece and Spain. But GBP/USD losses tempered by GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Data focus: 0830 GMT UK March industrial production & manufacturing output, 1400 GMT UK April NIESR GDP estimates. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode; 5-day moving average staged bearish crossover against 15-day MA. Support at 1.6067 (yesterday's low); breach would target 1.6035 (April 20 low), then 1.6005 (April 19 low), 1.5949 (55-day moving average) and 1.5833 (200-day moving average). Resistance at 1.6158 (yesterday's high), then at 1.6201 (Friday's high), 1.6215 (May 3 high) and 1.6238-1.6247 band (May 2 high-May 1 high); breach would expose upside to 1.6301 (April 30 eight-month high), then 1.6455 (Aug. 29 high) and 1.6570 (Aug. 23 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting near-two-month high of 0.9301 yesterday. Underpinned by broadly stronger demand for safe-haven USD as risk aversion mounts; heightened expectations of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as EUR/CHF hovers just above 1.2000 floor (cross last at 1.2007). But USD/CHF gains tempered by CHF demand on EUR/CHF cross. Daily chart positive-biased as 5-day moving average above 15-day MA and rising; MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought. Resistance at 0.9301 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9334-0.9340 band (March 15 high-Jan. 25 high), then 0.9393 (Jan. 23 high), 0.9573 (Jan. 17 high) and 0.9595 (11-month high when hit Jan. 9). Support at 0.9256 (hourly chart), then at 0.9230 (yesterday's low) and 0.9191 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9111 (Friday's low), then 0.9069 (May 2 low), 0.9039 (May 1 low) and 0.8998 (April 2 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to consolidate with positive bias after hitting 3.5-month high of 1.0062 yesterday. Underpinned by softer commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled down 20 cents yesterday at $96.81/bbl), worries about Greece and Spain. But USD/CAD gains tempered by hawkish \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  monetary policy stance. Data focus: 1230 GMT Canada March trade balance, new-housing price index. USD/CAD daily chart positive-biased as 5-day moving average above 15-day MA and rising; MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter near overbought. Resistance at 1.0062-1.0070 band (yesterday's high-Jan. 30 high); breach would expose upside to 1.0147 (Jan. 25 high), then 1.0162 (Jan. 23 high) and 1.0252 (Jan. 16 high). Support at 0.9998 (hourly chart), then at 0.9974 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 0.9920 (Tuesday's low), then 0.9858 (Friday's low), 0.9825 (May 3 low) and 0.9796 (April 27 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to consolidate with negative bias after hitting near-three-month low of 102.76 yesterday. Undermined by worries about Greece and Spain; Japan exporter sales. But EUR/JPY losses tempered by Japan importer demand. Daily chart negative-biased as