\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Japan Tries To Talk Down Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 950 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 2012\par 16:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo--The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's decision last week to again loosen credit has done little to weaken the yen, prompting the nation's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat to issue a rare on-the-record threat of countermeasures as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a two-month high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Even so, Tuesday's "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " failed to reverse the yen's rise amid doubts that Japanese authorities will follow up their words by stepping into the market soon.\par \par \uc2 But an acceleration in the yen's ascent could push Tokyo to consider actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely hinder economic growth, seen as crucial for the government to push through a planned sales tax hike, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 "We are concerned about the somewhat rapid appreciation of the yen since the end of last week," Takehiko Nakao, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters Tuesday, adding that such moves were a reflection of market speculation.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to closely monitor the market with caution so that we can act in a timely, appropriate manner when needed," he said.\par \par \uc2 But the vice finance minister's rare on-the-record comments failed to put the brakes on the yen, which hit Y79.64 to the dollar in Asian trading hours, its highest level in two months. The dollar traded at Y79.82 at the start of the New York day, unchanged from late Monday in New York. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell 3.6% against the yen during April, its largest monthly drop since July, amid concerns about the strength of the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Nakao's comments follow the BOJ's decision on Friday to increase its purchases of government bonds by Y10 trillion, or more than $125 billion, equaling the amount it announced in February, in a bid to overcome deflation and weaken the yen. But the move failed to reverse the yen's rise because the amount was within the range expected by analysts and traders.\par \par \uc2 Japanese bureaucrats tend to avoid the spotlight, but Mr. Nakao went out of his way to ensure that the market was aware of the finance ministry's stance, a government official familiar with the situation told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 "If you think the ministry is taking its eyes off the market, feeling there is little it can do about the yen's gain, you're wrong," the government official said.\par \par \uc2 The official said that while recent U.S. economic indicators may have been weak, policy makers in the ministry who watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves hold a "sense of discontent that this movement is overly one-sided."\par \par \uc2 The lack of reaction to Mr. Nakao's comments reflect the widely held market view that the dollar's current level, well above the postwar record low of Y75.31 hit Oct. 31, is still too high to prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling yen in an effort to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. Some market players also speculate that Japan may find it hard to actually step into the market, as that could possibly anger the administration of U.S. President Barack Obama, particularly ahead of November's presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 Falls in the yen benefit Japan's exporters at the expense of their rivals, including U.S. manufacturers. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  last August and October earned a reprimand from the U.S. Treasury Department. But the Japanese government official argued otherwise.\par \par \uc2 "Japan will act when it needs to," the government official said. "While this year may be very important for the U.S. administration, it is also politically very important for Japan."\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda has pledged to stake his political career on passing bills this year to double Japan's 5% sales tax by 2015, and their success rests partly on whether the economy can further recover, making it easier for lawmakers to sign off on the unpopular legislation. The fate of the bills is also likely to determine the timing of the next general election, which must happen by the summer of 2013.\par \par \uc2 When the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, during the five days through Nov. 4 last year, it sold an unprecedented Y9.1 trillion to buy dollars. That lifted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by around Y4, but only briefly. The fleeting result prompted some Japanese politicians to directly tell the finance ministry that the action was a waste of money and that monetary policy was far more powerful in affecting exchange rates. That has put more political pressure on the BOJ and led to the decision to buy more bonds.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, a BOJ decision in February to increase its purchases of government bonds by Y10 trillion and make a clear commitment to seek a 1% inflation goal took the markets by surprise and pushed the dollar above Y84 by mid-March. Including Friday's announcement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it would buy Y29 trillion by the end of June 2013, when the program is set to end.\par \par \uc2 But the finance ministry seems to believe \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has a key role to play, even as it welcomes any actions by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that help weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Last fall's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  resulted in the yen's stability during subsequent months by absorbing yen-buying orders from Japan's exporters, and helping scare away speculators, the government official said, adding: "The market knows its impact."\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi, The Wall Street Journal; Takashi.Nakamichi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 --Alex Martin contributed to this article. [ 05-01-12 1001ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120501e851000bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
