\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan's Top  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Bureaucrat Warns Against "Rapid" Yen Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 2012\par 06:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat warns that yen's rise since last week is rapid\par \par \uc2 -- Says government will take appropriate \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions when needed\par \par \uc2 -- Analyst discounts chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but if "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " continues, market could become more concerned\par \par \uc2 (Adds more quotes from vice finance minister in 4th-5th paragraphs, analyst comments, more background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Japanese government on Tuesday escalated its warnings about the yen after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose to a two-month high, with its top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy-making bureaucrat calling the yen's move "somewhat rapid" and renewing his pledge to act if necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The remarks from Takehiko Nakao, vice finance minister for international affairs, sparked little reaction from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market partly because they contained no hints of imminent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen, such as threats of "decisive steps."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But like many other Japanese bureaucrats, Nakao usually avoids discussing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issues on record, so his decision to speak out is a sign that Tokyo is getting more serious about talking down the yen after it touched Y79.73 to the dollar on Monday, the highest since Feb. 22.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are concerned about the somewhat rapid appreciation of the yen since the end of last week," which is "reflecting market speculation," Nakao told reporters at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "We will continue to closely monitor the market with caution so that we can act in a timely, appropriate manner when needed."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's government will remain "vigilant" against speculators trying to take advantage of low transaction volumes in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market amid the nation's Golden Week holidays to push the yen sharply higher, Nakao said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar has fallen roughly 2% versus the yen since Friday, putting pressure on Japan's still-fragile export-reliant economic recovery. Weak U.S. data and more signs of trouble in Europe over the debt crisis made the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more attractive to global investors, while traders viewed the recent monetary easing steps by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  as inadequate to send the yen lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government officials think the yen's rebound makes no good economic sense. One of them noted Tuesday that the market appears to underestimate the BOJ's commitment to monetary loosening, and that investors have yet to fully grasp weakness in the country's international trade performance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market doesn't seem to be conscious of any potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at this point," as the dollar remains comfortably above a post-war low of Y75.31 marked on Oct. 31, said Minori Uchida, a senior analyst at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  UFJ.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that day launched one of its most aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ever, dumping an unprecedented Y9.1 trillion in five days through Nov. 4.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But...considering Nakao's comments, that the government is more concerned with the momentum of the spot rate's decline rather than its actual levels," Uchida added. "If such comments continue, the market could become concerned of a potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Alex Martin contributed to this article. [ 05-01-12 0030ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120501e8510000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
