\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan in talks to buy South Korea debt to diversify reserves\b0\par\par\uc2 323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2012\par 01:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Announcement due next week - Japanese newspaper\par \par \uc2  * Japan agreed to buy China govt debt last month\par \par \uc2  * Other central banks also buying South Korea debt \par \par \uc2 (Recasts and writes through)\par \par \uc2  By Stanley White and Choonsik Yoo\par \par \uc2  TOKYO/SEOUL, April 27 (Reuters) - Japan is in talks with South Korea about buying its government bonds, senior officials from both countries said, a further sign that Tokyo it is beginning to diversify its reserves away from the dollar after agreeing to buy government debt from China last month.\par \par \uc2  Finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs from the two countries are due to meet in Manila on May 3 for Asian Development Bank meetings and Japan's Asahi newspaper said an announcement could be made then.\par \par \uc2  "Discussion has been under way for quite some time but I don't know about the report that an announcement will be made," South Korean Vice Finance Minister Shin Je-yoon said by telephone in Seoul.\par \par \uc2  Japan's reserves stood at $1.3 trillion at the end of last month, the second-largest after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Japan does not release a detailed breakdown but the bulk of its reserves are believed to be held in U.S. Treasuries as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has agreed to buy some $10 billion in Chinese government debt, the first major economy to do so, and a move which helps bolster the credibility of the yuan as an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  China, Switzerland, Thailand and Malaysia are among other central banks that have invested in South Korean local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, as they seek to diversify \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves away from U.S. dollar-denominated assets. \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Edwina Gibbs)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/KOREA (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | J | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z | U | RNP | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120426e84q001zt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
