\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Fin Min: Asked IMF To Give Japan Posts It Deserves At Institution\b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2012\par 20:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister on Friday touted the nation's decision to offer a $60 billion contribution to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and urged the fund to reward Tokyo by offering Japanese nationals good posts at the institution.\par \par \uc2 "I've told the IMF that we will not only provide it with money but also make human contributions to the fund so we want it to give us posts matching the levels of our contributions," Jun Azumi said at a news conference after a two-day meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs from the Group of 20 leading industrial and developing economies.\par \par \uc2 Azumi said he meant posts for young Japanese government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, not the managing director job currently held by \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But his unusually blunt call for positions at the IMF comes amid mounting concerns among Japanese government officials that Tokyo could lose an IMF deputy managing director post it now holds to a developing nation in several years.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 04-20-12 1604ET ]\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat Friday warned that the government is keeping an eye on the yen's exchange rate and will take steps as appropriate, in a sign of Tokyo's continued concerns over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 "There is no change in our situation. If the yen strengthens, that would pose downside risks to Japan's economy," Vice Finance Minister For International Affairs Takehiko Nakao said at a news conference after a two-day meeting of Group of 20 finance officials. "There is absolutely no change in our stance that we will continue to watch the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with a sense of caution and act as appropriate."\par \par \uc2 Nakao also emphasized that the G-20 leading industrialized and developing nations "share the view that excess volatility or disorderly movement in exchange rates could have adverse effects on economic and financial stability."\par \par \uc2 His comments are likely aimed at pre-empting speculation that other G-20 nations may have become more relaxed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, making it harder for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to justify direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's strength in the future when there is such need. The G-20 didn't clearly spell out concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility in its final statement following the group's meeting, released earlier Friday.\par \par \uc2 With the dollar holding above Y80, and with the U.S. and Europe seemingly opposed to market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , few investors expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will step into the market anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 04-20-12 1628ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120420e84k000hc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
