f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial Instruments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In accordance with its investment objectives and policies, the Company holds financial instruments which at any one time may comprise the following:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    *    securities held in accordance with the investment objectives and policies \par    *    cash and short-term debtors and creditors arising directly from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par    *    borrowing used to finance investment activity \par    *    derivative transactions including investment in warrants and forward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  contracts \par    *    options or futures for hedging purposes \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The financial instruments held by the Company principally comprise equities listed on the stock market in Japan. The specific risks arising from the Company's exposure to these instruments, and the Manager/Investment Advisor's policies for managing these risks, which have been applied throughout the year, are summarised below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital Management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company is a closed-ended investment company, and thus has a fixed capital. The Company's capital is represented by Ordinary Shares and each share carries one vote. They are entitled to dividends when declared. [ 18-04-12 0935GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As approved at the AGM on 22 August, 2011, the Company may purchase a maximum of 5,724,519 Ordinary Shares, equivalent to 5.80% of the Issued share capital of the Company as at the date of the AGM provided that;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --     The minimum price to be paid (exclusive of expenses) be US$0.01; \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The maximum price to be paid (exclusive of expenses) be 105% of the average mid-market valuation for five days preceding the purchase; and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- If the shares are trading on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  at a discount to the lower of the undiluted or diluted Net Asset Value;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board also considers from time to time whether it may be appropriate to raise new capital by a further issue of shares. The raising of new capital would however be dependent on there being genuine market demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company is not subject to externally imposed capital requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market Price Risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's investment portfolio - particularly its equity investments - is exposed to market price fluctuations, which are monitored by the Manager/Investment Advisor in pursuance of the investment objectives and policies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exceptional risks associated with investment in Japanese smaller companies may include:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 a) greater price volatility, substantially less liquidity and significantly smaller market capitalisation, and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    b)    more substantial government  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   in the economy,  including  restrictions  on investing in companies or in industries deemed sensitive to relevant national interests. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market price sensitivity analysis\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sensitivity of the Company to market price risk can be approximated by measuring the impact that a movement in the Topix Small Index would have on the percentage of funds invested. Topix Small Index is the capitalization-weighted index designed to measure the stocks not included in the Topix 500 Index that are listed on the First Section of the Tokyo Stock Exchange. Topix Small Index provides an indicator of the effect of market price risk on the Company's portfolio since its characteristics with respect to average market capitalization more closely resemble the investment strategy pursued by the Company. However, the Company's investments are concentrated to the real estate industry and unlisted and corporate bonds and as such do not reflect the full array of companies on the index. At 31 December, 2011 a 1% positive/negative movement in the index would produce a positive/negative movement in the net assets of the Company of US$389,663 (2010: US$322,648) for equity related securities. This relationship between the movement in the value of the assets of the Company and the Index is of a linear nature. As the intrinsic value of the corporate bonds is affected by the movements in interest rates, an increase in the interest rate would decrease the value of the bonds and a decrease would have an opposite effect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company principally invests in securities denominated in Yen rather than United States Dollar, the functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Company. Therefore, the Statement of Financial Position may be affected by movements in the exchange rates of such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the US Dollar. The Manager/Investment Advisor has the power to manage exposure to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements by using forward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  contracts. The Company was not party to any such instruments at the date of these Financial Statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is not the present intention of the Directors to hedge the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure of the Company, but the Directors reserve the right to do so in the future if they consider this to be desirable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The treatment of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions other than in US Dollars is set out in Note 1 to the Financial Statements under "Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure is as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                            31.12.2011   31.12.2010 \par                                                                 In US        In US \par                                                               Dollars      Dollars \par  Investments \par  Japanese Yen (Yen6,300,779,956, 2010:Yen7,105,869,103)    81,179,926   87,145,807 \par  Sterling (GBPNil, \par   2010:GBP34,888)                                                   -       56,315 \par  \par  \par  \par                                                            81,179,926   87,202,122 \par  \par  Other (Liabilities)/Assets \par  US Dollars                                                 (130,237)    (137,668) \par  Sterling (GBP40,938, \par   2010:GBP42,330)                                            (63,622)     (68,327) \par  Japanese Yen (Yen94,591,186, \par   2010:Yen152,447,021)                                      1,218,723    1,869,598 \par  \par  \par                                                             1,024,864    1,663,603 \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The below details the Company's sensitivity to a 10% (31 December 2010: 10%) change in the Yen exchange rates against the US Dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                       31.12.2011           31.12.2010 \par                                    In US Dollars        In US Dollars \par  Impact on Statement of Comprehensive Income \par   and Equity in response to a \par    - 10% increase                      7,497,147            8,093,510 \par                          =======================  =================== \par  \par  \par    - 10% decrease                    (9,161,768)          (9,891,804) \par                          =======================  =================== \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Interest Rate Risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company may invest in fixed and floating rate securities. The income of the Company may be affected by changes to interest rates relevant to particular securities or as a result of the Manager/Investment Advisor being unable to secure similar returns on the expiry of contracts or sale of securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The value of fixed interest securities may be affected by interest rate movements in the future however, in the Directors' opinion no material impact is expected. Interest receivable on bank deposits or payable on bank overdraft positions will be affected by fluctuations in interest rates, however the value of the underlying cash positions will not be affected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The direct effect of movements in interest rates are not material on cash and cash equivalent as the Company predominately keeps its surplus cash in Japanese Yen on which it does not earn interest.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Interest rate sensitivity analysis\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the intrinsic value of the corporate bonds is affected by the movements in interest rates, an increase in the interest rate would decrease the value of the corporate bonds and a decrease would have an opposite effect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fair Value\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All assets and liabilities are carried at fair value or at carrying value which equates to fair value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IFRS 7 requires the Company to classify fair value measurements using a fair value hierarchy that reflects the significance of the inputs used in making the measurements. The fair value hierarchy has the following levels:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (i)    Quoted prices (unadjusted) in active markets for identical assets or liabilities \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (level 1).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (ii) Inputs other than quoted prices included within level 1 that are observable for the asset or liability,