\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Will Be  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Only Option\b0\par\par\uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2012\par 04:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Easing policy stances at Fed, ECB and even RBA\par \par \uc2 -- Flows show Japanese investors bringing money home\par \par \uc2 -- BOJ would need to ease more than other central banks\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN\par \par \uc2 LONDON (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That certainly appears to be the case if Tokyo is serious about preventing another damaging rise in the value of the yen.\par \par \uc2 As each day passes, there is more reason to expect the yen's recent rebound will continue and that some steps, such as further monetary easing by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , simply won't be enough to force a reverse.\par \par \uc2 At least some of this growing support for the yen is coming from the rapidly deteriorating economic picture abroad.\par \par \uc2 Other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the dollar and the euro, are about to be undermined by easier monetary stances at the U.S. Federal Reserve as well as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even smaller higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Australian dollar, are losing their attraction.\par \par \uc2 In the Aussie's case, slower economic growth in China and an increasingly dovish stance from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  means there is little reason for Japanese investors to continue putting their money in Australia as well.\par \par \uc2 And that is the big problem--Japanese investors.\par \par \uc2 They appear to have kicked off the start of the new financial year with a concerted effort to bring their money home. New data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed that Y1.9 trillion of foreign bonds were sold in the first week of the new financial year. This was the highest weekly sell off in six months and took the total net sales in the last four weeks to Y1.5 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors were helping the yen too, by buying Y3.2 trillion of money market securities. Bank of New York Mellon, which tracks its own flow data, points to a particularly close correlation with flows in and out of the Australian dollar in recent months.\par \par \uc2 "It is important to note that not only have we seen renewed inflows into the yen since late March but we have started to monitor fresh outflows from the Australian dollar," according to Simon Derrick, one of the bank's senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists.\par \par \uc2 Although interest rates play a part in the investment decisions of the Japanese, Derrick notes that slowing growth in China, weaker data from the U.S., renewed fears of turbulence in the euro zone and political uncertainty in the Middle East as well as North Korea are factors encouraging the Japanese to bring their money home, once again cementing the yen's safe haven status.\par \par \uc2 Over the last few days, Japanese officials have been only too keen to encourage speculation that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will ease policy again as early as April 27.\par \par \uc2 But if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only going to be seen moving in lock step with easing patterns elsewhere, this is unlikely to reverse the current direction of investment flows. The yen will continue rising and Tokyo will have little option but to step into the market and sell its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop it rising any more.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 Reuters USD/DJ\par \par \uc2 Thomson P/1066 or P/1074\par \par \uc2 (Nicholas Hastings is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires who has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com or on twitter @NickHastingsDJ)\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120414e84d000wb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
