\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2012\par 05:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ TANKAN SHOWS BIG COS STILL CAUTIOUS, EXPECT YEN TO STAY STRONG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's closely watched tankan survey showed Monday that corporate managers remained cautious in the three months to March, as the yen's fall from historic highs and the fading impact of supply-chain disruptions from last year's Thai floods did little to cheer sentiment. (published 0012 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVT FUJIMURA: HIGH OIL PRICES A CONCERN FOR ECON RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's top government spokesman on Monday expressed concern that high oil prices and a potential global economic slowdown could inhibit the nation's economic recovery. (published 0238 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ITALY PRIME MINISTER: ECONOMY STABILIZED, BUT MAY STILL POSE EURO ZONE RISK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian Prime Minister Mario Monti Monday said Italy's economy has stabilized, but it may still pose a risk to the euro zone. (published 0237 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: WEAK AUSTRALIA HOUSING DEMAND TO DRAG ON ECONOMIC GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian building approvals were much weaker than expected in February sending a clear signal that interest rate cuts in late 2011 have failed to spur demand with housing set to drag on economic growth in the first quarter. (published 0255 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN MANUFACTURING ACTIVITY CONTRACTS IN MARCH AS A$ BITES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A crisis in Australian manufacturing deepened in March with an index of activity across the besieged sector showing it contracted. (published 2331 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMANY SEES 2012 BUDGET DEFICIT AT 0.9% OF GDP - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's Finance Minister, Wolfgang Schaeuble, has notified the E.U. Commission that the country's 2012 budget deficit is likely to amount to 0.9% of gross domestic product, German news magazine website Focus Online said Sunday. The magazine cited the official April report that the finance ministry gave to the commission. (published 0136 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ YAMAGUCHI: BOJ PROVIDING AMPLE YEN FUNDS INTO MKT WHILE LEAVING \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FUNDS UNSTERILIZED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deputy Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hirohide Yamaguchi\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Monday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has injected ample funds into the banking system, while leaving the funds obtained from yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unsterilized. (published 0235 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA VICE PREMIER: CONFIDENT OF RELATIVELY FAST ECONOMIC GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese Vice Premier Li Keqiang said Monday he is "confident" in the country's ability to maintain long-term, stable and relatively fast economic growth. (published 0207 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: CHINA WOULD BENEFIT FROM FREER YUAN - ADB VICE PRESIDENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A freer floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be good for China as it will give the country's monetary authorities more control over their policies, a vice president of the Asian Development Bank said. (published 0255 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  PMI AT ONE-YEAR HIGH OF 52.0 IN MARCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's manufacturing activity expanded for a second straight month, with a key index reaching a one-year high, backed by a rise in new orders, according to data released by \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Monday. (published 0021 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: S KOREA MAR CONSUMER PRICES UP 2.6% ON YEAR, DOWN 0.1% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's consumer inflation unexpectedly eased for a third straight month to a 20-month low in March, government data showed Monday. (published 2316 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE 1Q PRIVATE RESIDENTIAL PRICES FALL 0.1% ON QUARTER VS +0.2% IN 4Q11 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices of private homes in Singapore fell 0.1% in the first quarter from the previous three-month period, compared to a 0.2% rise in the fourth quarter of last year, marking their first decline after nine quarters of growth, according to flash estimates issued by the government Monday. (published 0045 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TAIWAN \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  MARCH PMI 54.1 VS 52.7 FEBRUARY - MARKIT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Taiwan Purchasing Managers Index rose in March to 54.1 from 52.7 in February as overall new orders rose on increased demand, both at home and overseas, index compiler Markit Group said Monday. (published 0207 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par MONEY WEEK AHEAD: TREASURY INVESTORS RETHINK FED'S RATE TALK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Burned by their misinterpretation of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  rhetoric, U.S. Treasury investors have backed off their once-steadfast outlook for a monetary-policy holding pattern. (published 2200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-04-12 0301GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120402e8420004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
