\par\par \b \uc2 COMMENT: Please (don't) stop loving me\b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 13:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 By Divyang Shah\par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 16 (IFR) - In a world where safe havens are still in strong demand the Norges bank has joined a growing group trying to prevent its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from finding too much safe haven-related love. We don't think it likely that the Norges bank will be successful in making the NOK less attractive -- not unless it was willing to adopt zero interest rates and start to more actively target the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 CHOOSING BETWEEN THE UGLY SISTERS:\par \par \uc2 The ugly sisters competition between the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs continues with the volatility between EUR, USD and GBP likely to remain as related central banks keep the liquidity flowing and print money. The drive toward quantitative/credit easing from the ECB, Fed and BoE has forced investors to contemplate less liquid but perceived-as harder \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they look to maintain the store of value of their holdings.\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD and Cable are likely to meander in wide ranges as the markets move from one ugly sister to the next and back again. This is a merry-go-round that will continue for sometime but more generally the trend toward other safer and harder \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue. Two such safe havens (CHF and JPY) have been the target of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action and now we could be joined by a third (NOK).\par \par \uc2 LESSON FROM BOJ AND SNB:\par \par \uc2 It was \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  which initially, through \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  combined with QE, tried to put a floor on JPY-strength. Early this year the strategy was given more credibility as the BoJ adopted an inflation goal/target which made it likely that further more aggressive QE will be delivered.\par \par \uc2 Unlike the BoJ the SNB took a more direct approach by placing a direct floor on CHF strength at 1.20 against the EUR, choosing the middle road between an exchange rate target below 1.20 and an inflation target above 1.20.\par \par \uc2 What the BoJ and SNB action shows is that ultimately safe haven demand is not about investors seeking a return but a desire to preserve the store of value. The lack of sensitivity to return means that interest rate changes do not matter, which would explain why the SNB was unsuccessful in its initial attempts to deal with CHF strength via lower rates. Also, when you do play around with perceptions over the store of value, then you need to make credible threats and the BoJ was only successful after it installed an inflation goal/target.\par \par \uc2 NOK TO REMAIN SAFE HAVEN, CHF TO MAKE A COMEBACK:\par \par \uc2 As long as Norges bank does not show willingness to print money either through QE or through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , then the NOK will remain a safe haven. For now, the reaction function of the Norges bank is to greet further NOK strength with lower interest rates, and while we wait for this theme to be exhausted the NOK will continue to strengthen to new highs vs the EUR. But the lesson for the BoJ and SNB is also that if they are perceived as having gone soft in their willingness to stick to the exchange rate target, then the markets will once again focus on the safe haven trade.\par \par \uc2 It is in this context we found it interesting that the SNB focused on the "mortgage and real estate market for residential property" at their March meeting where they saw "growing signs of imbalances". This is a new inclusion of risks which was missing from the December and September meetings and the first time the SNB has shown a potential weakness in its drive to enforce a minimum level for EUR/CHF at 1.20 with "utmost determination".\par \par \uc2 (Divyang Shah is a global market strategist for IFR Markets, based in London)\par \par \uc2 SAFEHAVEN-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2 / | ABN | D | M   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120319e83g000pn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
