\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Sends Out Warning On Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 03:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO -- Japan sent out a warning Monday that the country has not let its guard down against the yen's strength despite a recent weakening in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , underscoring Tokyo's continued vigilance against speculators trying to push the yen higher.\par \par \uc2 Speaking in parliament, Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda said that the yen is still "strong," while Finance Minister Jun Azumi renewed his pledge to act against any "excessively speculative movements."\par \par \uc2 The remarks show that Japanese officials remain on alert over any possible rebound in the yen, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens from its record high against the dollar hit last year. A strong yen hurts Japan's economy by making its exports less competitive overseas.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already concluded that there won't be any surge of yen-buying at the end of this month on the back of Japanese firms' repatriation flows, a government official told Dow Jones Newswires, citing the results of a ministry survey of firms' repatriation plans.\par \par \uc2 That means Tokyo may view any sharp rises in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next few weeks as driven by speculation--something it has vowed to fight through market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed.\par \par \uc2 Although analysts think the U.S.'s recent reprimand of Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigns last year could discourage further forays into the market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past even when there was no U.S. or European support.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the finance ministry conducted a non-publicized survey to examine Japanese companies' repatriation plans in the run-up to their book-closing on March 31, when the country's financial year ends, the government official told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Some investors expect companies to buy the yen to bring back foreign revenue, but the official said that many of the firms contacted by the ministry--including manufacturers, banks and trading companies--said they had no such plans or had already converted their overseas profits using tools such as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward agreements.\par \par \uc2 The ministry's conclusion is that "any large demand for the yen isn't expected to emerge during March" through repatriation, the official said. He offered no details on estimated repatriation flows or the number of firms polled.\par \par \uc2 The MOF is also likely to extend its measure to keep track of traders' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bets beyond the planned expiration this month, government officials said.\par \par \uc2 "That's probably going to be extended, as things are finally starting to move in a positive direction," one of them told Dow Jones.\par \par \uc2 The dollar on Friday touched a 10-month high of Y82.65. At around 1100 GMT it stood at Y82.24.\par \par \uc2 Bullish sentiment toward the yen has recently weakened on poor Japanese trade data, the Bank of Japan's additional monetary easing and receding concerns over Europe's debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6895-7558; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 - Kosaku Narioka contributed to this article [ 03-11-12 2245ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020120312e83c0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
