\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Fin Min Told G-20 That Tokyo Will Take Decisive Steps As Needed On Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2012\par 03:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister says he repeated standard line on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at G-20 meeting\par \par \uc2 -- Characterizes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue discussions at G-20 as "not intense"\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Kelly Olsen \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Adds more comments from finance minister in 2nd, 4th paragraphs, background throughout)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister reiterated at a weekend meeting of Group of 20 finance ministers and central bankers that Tokyo will take action as needed in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to combat excessive and speculative-driven movements in the yen, the minister said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As I have previously said, Japan will take decisive measures in the face of excessive movements and speculatively excessive moves in such a case," Jun Azumi said at a press conference following Tuesday's Cabinet meeting. Azumi added that he repeated that stance at the most recent G-20 meeting just as he had at the previous gathering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of 20 industrialized and developing economies met in Mexico City, and while their discussions focused on the European debt crisis and how to deal with it, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are a sensitive issue that have drawn criticism from some G-20 nations, including close ally the United States.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Azumi said that discussion of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issue at the meeting was "not intense" following the Bank of Japan's monetary policy easing steps announced earlier this month as well as the stabilizing situation surrounding Europe's debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 G-20 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors agreed at their Mexico meeting that Europe must add more funds to its so-called firewall for dealing with the region's sovereign debt crisis before G-20 member countries can boost financial support for the International Monetary Fund to help deal with it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar, which had been trading near a record low against the yen, has strengthened since the Bank of Japan boosted the size of its asset purchase program on Feb. 14 in a move seen as largely helping the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken versus the greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was trading at Y80.16 at 0158 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance ministry sold Y8.072 trillion on Oct. 31 last year via the BOJ in its biggest ever single-day \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the dollar fell to a record low of Y75.31 against the yen. It continued to order yen sales covertly for four days afterward, ministry data released earlier this month showed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Kelly Olsen, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2790; kelly.olsen@dowjones.com [ 28-02-12 0211GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120228e82s0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
