\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Falls To Seven-Month Low Against Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Walter   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2012\par 19:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -(\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )- The yen fell to Y80 against the dollar for the first time since August, hammered by the Bank of Japan's stepped-up efforts to revive the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has tumbled every day since Feb. 14, when the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a surprise announcement it would add Y10 trillion to its asset-buying program.\par \par \uc2 The aim is to stimulate Japan's economy, which contracted by 2.3% in the fourth quarter, and last year posted its first annual trade deficit since 1980.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's efforts put downward pressure on the yen by increasing the money supply. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly three times last year to weaken the yen, which hit a post-World War II record high of Y75.31 in October.\par \par \uc2 The yen was down 0.79% at Y80.38 to the dollar Wednesday afternoon in New York. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is off over 5% in February, and its intraday low of Y80.40 was its weakest point since July 11.\par \par \uc2 With the Bank of Japan shaking its conservative reputation, some in the market believe a yen rally that stretches back to 1998 is coming to an end.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  lowered its forecast for the yen to Y85 against the dollar by the end of the year, compared with its previous estimate of Y80. The bank expects the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue to slide in 2013, to Y90.\par \par \uc2 "With the yen, we have a whole host of themes coming together at the same time" that are pulling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down, said Camilla Sutton, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Scotia Capital in Toronto. Sutton sees the yen ending the year at Y82 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 However, others see a more limited downside for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many investors bought the yen as a safe harbor last year when Europe's debt crisis threatened to undermine the global economy. They could return if efforts to avoid a Greek default falter.\par \par \uc2 Japan is also likely to return to a trade surplus in 2012, as the effects of last year's earthquake fade, said Bilal Hafeez, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "A lot of investors want to see the yen weakened on a sustained basis, but I don't think that's going to happen yet," Hafeez said, adding that the yen will strengthen "unless Japan is going to continually step up and do more [easing]."\par \par \uc2 -By Matthew Walter, \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\uc2 ; 212-416-2910; matt.walter@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Martin Vaughan and Jessica Mead contributed to this article [ 02-22-12 1331ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020120222e82m00073\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
