\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2012\par 01:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The euro strengthened Friday on hopes that the complex negotiations over a new debt relief package for Greece were coming together ahead of a Monday meeting of euro-zone finance ministers. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rallied 4.2% against the dollar since touching its lowest level in more than 16 months in January. The dollar had its second weekly gain against the Japanese yen, climbing to its highest level since the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last October. Improving economic economic data in the U.S. and efforts by the Bank of Japan to stimulate its economy by increasing liquidity are both helping to support the dollar. The euro was at $1.3139 from $1.3130 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y79.58 from Y78.93 and at CHF0.9200 from CHF0.9190. The U.K. pound was at $1.5828 from $1.5799. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at about 79.392 from about 79.348.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting one-week high of 1.3223 this morning on expectations that euro-zone finance ministers will approve second EUR130 billion Greece bailout package and EUR200 billion debt restructuring at meeting starting at 1430 GMT today in Brussels. "I don't think there is a majority for another path," said Austrian Finance Minister Maria Fekter in a television interview Sunday. But euro sentiment dented by news that the IMF will contribute less to this second Greek rescue package than originally planned, providing just EUR13 billion of the EUR130 billion aid as the global fund restricts its exposure to the European debt crisis and leaving the bloc's governments to provide a much bigger share of the loans than they did in the euro zone's 3 earlier bailouts. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turn bullish. Resistance at 1.3284 (Feb. 13 high); breach would expose upside to 1.3322 (Feb. 9 two-month high, near 100-day moving average), then 1.3436 (50% Fibonacci correction of 1.4248-1.2624 Oct. 27-Jan. 16 decline). Support at 1.3139 (hourly chart), then at 1.3115 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.2974 (Thursday's reaction low), then 1.2931 (Jan. 25 low) and 1.2854 (Jan. 23 low).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting 16-week high of 79.84 this morning. Liquidity will be thin later in global day as U.S. markets closed today for Presidents' Day holiday. Spotlight on 2350 GMT Japan January provisional trade statistics: country expected to post record Y1.468 trillion deficit, adding to the gloom over the nation's export-reliant economy. USD/JPY also underpinned by yen-funded carry trades amid positive global risk sentiment on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcement Saturday it will cut banks' reserve requirement ratio by 0.5 percentage point effective Feb. 24; hopes that a deal that foresees a loan of EUR130 billion to Greece and private creditors foregoing EUR100 billion of their sovereign debt holdings could be around the corner as euro-zone finance ministers meet in Brussels at 1430 GMT today. USD/JPY also supported by last week's BOJ's decision to expand its asset purchase program; Japan importer demand. But USD sentiment dented by lower-than-expected U.S. Conference Board January index of leading economic indicators (rose 0.4% vs +0.5% forecast) and less-than-expected U.S. January CPI of +0.2% (vs +0.3% forecast). USD/JPY gains also tempered by Japan exporter sales. USD/JPY daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode, although latter at overbought; 5-day moving average above 15-day MA and rising. Resistance at psychological 80.00 line; breach would target 80.25 (Aug. 4, 2011 high), then 81.48 (July 8, 2011 high), 81.77 May 31, 2011 high), 82.33 (May 19, 2011 high) and 82.77 (April 27, 2011 high). Support at 79.35 (hourly chart), then at 78.98 (hourly chart) and 78.79 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 78.36 (Thursday's low), then 78.18 (Wednesday's low), 78.00 (200-day moving average) and 77.36 (Tuesday's low, near 55-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with risks skewed higher after hitting one-week high of 1.5878 this morning. Supported by positive global risk sentiment; surprise 0.9% on-month rise in UK January retail sales. But GBP/USD gains tempered by GBP sales on buoyant EUR/GBP cross. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics in bullish mode. Resistance at 1.5884 (Feb. 9 high); breach would target 1.5914 (200-day moving average), then 1.5928 (Feb. 8 12-week high), psychological 1.6000 line and 1.6092 (Nov. 11 high). Support at 1.5797-1.5786 band (hourly chart-Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.5654 (Thursday's low), then 1.5642 (Tuesday's reaction low), 1.5610 (55-day moving average) and 1.5531 (Jan. 25 low).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate with risks skewed lowher after hitting one-week low of 0.9133 this morning. Undermined by broadly weaker demand for safe-haven USD as investor risk appetite increases on China's bank reserve requirement ratio cut and progress in Europe toward second bailout deal for Greece. But USD/CHF losses tempered by expectations of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics have turned bearish. Support at 0.9099 (Feb. 13 low); breach would target 0.9085 (Feb. 9 10-week low), then 0.9061 (Nov. 30 low), psychological 0.9000 line and 0.8950 (Nov. 11 low). Resistance at 0.9206 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9300 (Thursday's high), then 0.9339 (Jan. 25 high), 0.9388 (Jan. 23 high), 0.9412 (Jan. 19 high) and 0.9496 (Jan. 18 high).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to consolidate with risks skewed lower after hitting three-month low of 0.9914 this morning. Undermined by positive global risk sentiment; buoyant commodity & oil prices; higher-than-expected Canada January CPI. USD/CAD daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics in bearish mode; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.9937 this morning. Support at 0.9910 (Oct. 31 low); breach would target 0.9888 (Oct. 27 swing low), then 0.9776 (Sept. 19 reaction low). Resistance at 0.9984 (Friday's high), then at 1.0051 (Thursday's high); breach would target 1.0070 (Jan. 30 high), then 1.0147 (Jan. 25 high), 1.0162 (Jan. 23 high) and 1.0177-1.0188 band (Jan. 18 high-Jan. 17 high).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - - to trade in higher range. Buoyed by positive global risk sentiment as China on Saturday cut its banks' reserve requirement ratio by 50 bps and euro-zone finance ministers expected today to approve second Greece bailout package and debt restructuring. AUD/USD also supported by buoyant commodity prices (CRB spot index closed up 0.98 Friday at 317.39); AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics have turned bullish. Resistance at 1.0798 (Friday's high); breach would target 1.0824 (Feb. 9 high), then 1.0844 (Feb. 8 six-month high), psychological 1.1000 line and 1.1080 (July 27 top). Support at 1.0684 (Friday's low), then at 1.0642 (Thursday's low) and 1.0625 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0564 (Jan. 31 low), then 1.0521 (Jan. 30 low) and 1.0424 (Jan. 24 low).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to consolidate with risks skewed higher after hitting three-trading-day high of 0.8416 this morning. Supported by positive global risk sentiment; buoyant commodity prices; NZD-USD yield gap. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turning bullish. Resistance at 0.8421 (Wednesday's five-month high), then 0.8449 (Sept. 5 high); breach would expose upside to 0.8571 (Aug. 31 reaction high). Support at 0.8314 (Friday's low), then at 0.8240 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8207 (Feb. 1 low), then 0.8167 (Jan. 31 low), 0.8151 (Jan. 30 low), 0.8070 (200-day moving average), 0.8034 (Jan. 25 low) and 0.7990 (Jan. 20 low).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan rose against the U.S. dollar late Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher with a soft daily fixing for the greenback. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.2991 around 0830 GMT, down from Thursday's close of CNY6.3016. It traded in a small range of CNY6.2963 to CNY6.3007. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the dollar-yuan central parity rate at a low 6.2951, down from Thursday's 6.2991, following the dollar's weakness overseas.\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120220e82k0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
