\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: DJIA Rises Amid Greece Bailout Talks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,909 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2012\par 23:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA               12949.87     +45.79     +0.35 % \par Nasdaq              2951.78      -8.07     -0.27 % \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P 500\f28 \uc2 \f2              1361.23      +3.19     +0.23 % \par FTSE 100            5905.07     +19.69     +0.33 % \par Xetra DAX           6848.03     +96.07     +1.42 % \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 30/32        flat 0.286 \par 5Y Tsy   100 2/32        flat 0.864 \par 10Y Tsy  99 29/32        2.009%       +1.6BP \par  \par  \par USD/JPY Japan         78.92-94  +0.73% \par Range                    79.60-78.80 \par EUR/USD Euro         1.3131-35  +0.14% \par Range                    1.3197-1.3116 \par AUD/USD Australia    1.0752-57  -0.44% \par Range                    1.0798-1.0690 \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,725.90    -$2.50 \par Comex Gold Range       $1,718.60-$1,737.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $103.24    +$0.93 \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average pushed higher for the second day after European leaders signaled progress toward an agreement on a second bailout for Greece.\par \par \uc2 Both the Dow and \uc2 Standard & Poor's 500\uc2  stock indexes made runs at multiyear milestones, but a late rally lost momentum ahead of the long, holiday weekend. The Dow advanced 45.79 points, or 0.4%, to 12949.87, within striking distance of 13000, a threshold last hit in May 2008.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index rose 3.19 points, or 0.2%, to 1361.23, just short of its best close in almost four years. The Nasdaq Composite fell 8.07 points, or 0.3%, to 2951.78.\par \par \uc2 Consumer-discretionary and financial stocks were the strongest performers in the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  on a percentage basis, while health-care and utility stocks lagged. Intel was the biggest percentage gainer in the Dow, rising 55 cents, or 2%, to $27.37, while \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  fell 13 cents, or 1.3%, to 10.15.\par \par \uc2 The Stoxx Europe 600 climbed 0.6%, trading near levels not seen since last summer, after political leaders from Germany, Italy and Greece voiced confidence that a deal on a Greek bailout could be reached by Monday.\par \par \uc2 Developments in Europe failed to spark commensurate gains in U.S. stocks, though benchmarks drifted to session highs late in afternoon trade.\par \par \uc2 "While it looks like the pieces are slowly falling into place for Greece, there are a number of things that are not fully known. Some investors seem to be tentative going into a long weekend when a lot can happen," said Alan Gayle, senior investment strategist at RidgeWorth Investments.\par \par \uc2 Developments in Europe this weekend will color next week's trading, with fourth-quarter earnings season nearly finished and few major U.S. economic data reports on the agenda next week.\par \par \uc2 Asian bourses also were broadly higher, with Japan's Nikkei Stock Average climbing 1.6% to a six-month high. Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index advanced 1%.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures lost $2.30 per troy ounce, or 0.13%, to $1724.50, while crude-oil prices settled up 93 cents, or 0.9%, at $103.23 a barrel. The dollar lost ground against the euro and strengthened against the yen. The yield on the 10-year Treasury note rose to 2.012%.\par \par \uc2 U.S. economic data showed consumer prices rose in line with expectations in January. The newly composed index of leading economic indicators in January increased for the fourth consecutive month, just shy of expectations.\par \par \uc2 In corporate news, \uc2 H.J. Heinz\uc2  tacked on 2.37, or 4.6%, to 54.47 after the food company reported better-than-expected fiscal third-quarter earnings and revenue, and provided an upbeat full-year earnings outlook.\par \par \uc2 General Mills\uc2  shed 1.44, or 3.6%, 38.34 after it indicated fiscal third-quarter earnings would be below estimates and lowered its full-year outlook, citing weak volumes across its U.S. retail categories in December and January.\par \par \uc2 Gilead Sciences\uc2  slumped 7.81, or 14%, to 47.00, making it the weakest performer on the Nasdaq 100 and the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2 , after the company announced disappointing results to a study of its hepatitis C treatment.\par \par \uc2 Campbell Soup\uc2 's fiscal second-quarter earnings topped estimates while revenue were a bit shy. The company affirmed its full-year earnings and sales growth outlook. The stock rose 84 cents, or 2.6%, to 32.90.\par \par \uc2 First Solar climbed 2.88, or 7.3%, to 42.59 after Los Angeles County agencies approved changes to a construction permit required to build a large solar farm and complete a $1.36 billion deal with Exelon, which fell 23 cents, or 0.6%, to 39.27.\par \par \uc2 SunPower shot up 65 cents, or 8.7%, to 8.13 after the solar-panel maker reported a surprise fourth-quarter profit and revenue that was well above expectations.\par \par \uc2 Allscripts Healthcare Solutions\uc2  swung to a fourth-quarter profit on prior-year charges related to its acquisition of Eclipsys. Shares fell 1.93, or 9%, 19.54, however, after the company gave a cautious profit view for the current quarter.\par \par \uc2 Qlik Technologies\uc2 ' fourth-quarter earnings jumped 47% as the software company reported broad revenue growth. However, shares slid 2.58, or 8.2%, to 28.73 after Qlik's adjusted profit missed its expectations, and the company issued a downbeat first-quarter outlook.\par \par \uc2 Aruba Networks' fiscal second-quarter loss widened as the WiFi-equipment maker was hurt by higher charges. Shares fell 2.47, or 10%, to 22.18.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 The euro strengthened Friday on hopes that the complex negotiations over a new debt relief package for Greece were coming together ahead of a Monday meeting of euro-zone finance ministers. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rallied 4.2% against the dollar since touching its lowest level in more than 16 months in January.\par \par \uc2 Reports that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchanged Greek bonds Friday to ensure that its holdings wouldn't be subject to the same restructuring planned for private bond holders bolstered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Though some investors complained that the special treatment for the ECB would discourage further investment in euro zone bonds by subordinating private sector creditors, most took the mere presence of a deal as a sign of progress in the long-stalled talks to resolve Greece's debt problems.\par \par \uc2 The euro has traded in a relatively narrow range in recent weeks as talks have dragged on between Greece, its public sector European creditors, and private bondholders over a EUR130 billion bailout needed for the country to avoid a default next month.\par \par \uc2 "This is being read by the market as paving the way for a restructuring next week, so that was supportive, but it's still not 100% that its going to happen," said Ron Leven, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.3139 from $1.3130 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at Y104.54 from Y103.67.\par \par \uc2 After months of painstaking negotiations fraught with constant delays, investors remain cautious about next week's meetings. Even if negotiators are able to achieve a deal, a stagnating European economy, coupled with the possibility of more debt talks in other peripheral European countries, may weigh on the euro in the coming weeks, said Marc Chandler, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . He sees the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening to $1.20 by the middle of the year.\par \par \uc2 "Even if this program works, you are looking at a prolonged period of weak growth in Europe, and you are looking at further expansion of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet," he said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar had its second weekly gain against the Japanese yen, climbing to its highest level since the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last October. Improving economic economic data in the U.S. and efforts by the Bank of Japan to stimulate its economy by increasing liquidity are both helping to support the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Conference Board reported Friday that its index of leading economic indicators in January increased for the fourth consecutive month, and the Labor Department said that consumer prices rose 0.2% last month, less than expected.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was at Y79.58 from Y78.93 and at CHF0.9200 from CHF0.9190.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 Nurtured by some hopes that a bailout deal for Greece could be around the corner, investors rotated out of safe-harbor Treasury bonds Friday and embraced U.S. stocks.\par \par \uc2 But the bond market recouped part of the price losses in the afternoon session, signaling the sentiment in the financial markets was cautiously optimistic rather than outright euphoric given the on-and-off drama that has dragged on for months.\par \par \uc2 Friday, investors took some comfort in news reports that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  swapped its holdings of Greek government bonds with new ones, paving the way for approval of a fresh fund for Greece Monday by the European Union. Greece needs the new cash to avoid a default in March that analysts have warned could spark a new financial crisis with broad ripple effects on the global economy, a fear that has gripped markets for months.\par \par \uc2 "There continues to be strong hope of a Greek deal this Monday so we are seeing longs in the Treasury market being forced out in front of the three day weekend," said Jason Rogan, director of U.S. government bond trading in New York at \uc2 Guggenheim Partners LLC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A long is a bet on further price gains in bonds. The Treasury market will be shut Monday for the U.S. Presidents' Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 In late-afternoon trade, the benchmark 10-year note was 4/32 lower to yield 2.009%. The 30-year bond was 3/32 lower to yield 3.155%. The two-year note was flat to yield 0.286%. Bond prices move inversely to their yields. [ 17-02-12 2256GMT ]\par \par \uc2 The bond market also faces $99 billion in new debt sales in the coming week, including $35 billion in two-year notes, $35 billion in five-year notes and $29 billion in seven-year notes.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Greek Bailout Seen Within Reach \par US Blue-chip Stocks Climb With Focus On Greece \par US Consumer Prices Rise 0.2%, Largest Gain Since Sept \par TNT Express Rejects Offer From UPS \par EFSF Launches Facility To Boost Leverage \par Goldman Unit, Advent To Buy Transunion For $3b \par Terror Suspect Arrested Near US Capitol \par Payroll Tax Cut Pact Clears Congress \par Apple Paid China Company For Ipad Name \par Obama To Announce Matched Financing For Exporters \par  \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN ASIA-PACIFIC EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2145  NZ   Q4      PPI \par 2145  NZ   Q4      Capital Goods Price Index \par 2350  JPN  Jan     Provisional Trade Statistics for the Month \par 0030  AUS  Jan     International Merchandise Imports \par 0230  THA  Q4      GDP \par 0500  JPN  Jan     Steel Production \par 0500  JPN  Dec     Indexes of Business Conditions - Revision \par 0530  JPN  Jan     Tokyo area department store 