\par\par \b \uc2 Japan  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Officials Keep Guard Up Despite Yen Fall\b0\par\par\uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2012\par 11:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials remain on guard against yen gains.\par \par \uc2 -- The market is divided on whether the yen's uptrend is over.\par \par \uc2 -- Japan's behind-closed-doors dispute with the IMF shows Tokyo's conviction that its recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  make economic sense.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--The yen's recent fall may have given the Japanese finance ministry some breathing space, but the fact that some investors remain bullish on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also given officials a good reason to stay on their toes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since the Bank of Japan took the markets by surprise Tuesday by introducing a 1% inflation goal and pledged to buy more bonds through an asset-purchasing program, the dollar has risen about 2.4% against the yen, hitting a fresh three-and-a-half-month high of Y79.18 in Asian trading Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We very much welcome the BOJ's policy, and its effects are being seen in current market movements," a senior Japanese government official told \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market moves on various factors. We maintain a sense of tension, and we are watching \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements carefully," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A falling yen is a relief for Japan's struggling economy as it makes the nation's exports more competitive in global markets. But the yen's prospects are the subject of fierce debate, and the uncertainty is keeping the Japanese officials nervous.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the BOJ action and as Japan's trade account falling into a deficit, some investors, such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 , see the yen's long-running uptrend coming to an end.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Others say the prospects of further U.S. monetary easing and deterioration in Europe's debt crisis could rekindle the yen's gains. JPMorgan Chase Bank, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  expect the yen to climb in coming months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some yen-bulls are also skeptical of whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has the will to unilaterally \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to sell the yen, after the U.S. Treasury Department and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  both signaled firm opposition to such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over past months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Tokyo appears to be convinced that their solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , most recently in November, were justified, as shown by a recent behind-the-door dispute with the International Monetary Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials lodged a strong protest with the IMF after the fund's deputy director of the Asia and Pacific Department, Jerald Schiff, said in January that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "should be limited to periods of heightened exchange-rate volatility."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The remarks left an impression that he was critical of Tokyo's actions, Japanese officials told \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2 . As a result of their protest, the IMF issued a separate statement on Feb. 3 clarifying its position, they said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In that statement, Anoop Singh--the IMF's Asia and Pacific Department Director and Schiff's superior--called the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market "volatile" and described the yen as having "appreciated significantly."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The IMF said the statement was just to announce a regular IMF staff visit to Tokyo. The fund, however, usually doesn't issue a statement ahead of such visits to Japan at that time of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 17-02-12 1014GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120217e82h000lg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
