\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Shares Rise; US Data, Greece Optimism; Tokyo Hits 6-Mo High\b0\par\par\uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2012\par 05:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Stocks buoyant on positive U.S. data, optimism over Greece; Tokyo, Hong Kong and Seoul soar to over 6-month highs\par \par \uc2 -- Yen continues to weaken, buoys long-suffering Japanese exporters,\par \par \uc2 -- Upbeat moods lifts Australian dollar sharply against yen, while copper prices jump\par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels.)\par \par \uc2 By Shri Navaratnam and Puja Rajeev\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets were higher Friday on upbeat U.S. economic data and signs of progress in Greece's efforts to avoid a debt default, led by major markets such as Hong Kong and Tokyo scaling six-month highs, with Japanese exporters extending their rally on a rare period of yen weakness.\par \par \uc2 Investors were encouraged by news the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to swap its Greek debt holdings for new bonds once debt-restructuring negotiations are complete, moving Athens a step closer to securing a second bailout package critical to avoiding a default.\par \par \uc2 Copper prices rose 1.1%, and the Australian dollar -- a barometer of regional risk -- advanced against the safe-haven yen, supported by a batch of U.S. housing, jobless and manufacturing data showing strength in the world's biggest economy.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. continues to be one of the brighter spots in the global economy," \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said in a note. It added that while risks abound, "Greece is less likely to deliver a scare to markets."\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average rose 1.7% to 9394.15, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 tacked on 0.3%, South Korea's Kospi Composite jumped 1.4% Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index rose 0.7% to 21,426.7, China's Shanghai Composite Index fell 0.2% and India's Sensex jumped 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 DJIA futures were off two points in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 A liquidity-driven rally in stocks lifted Hong Kong's market to 21,546.73, its highest level since Aug. 4. Big caps that rose over-2% included Henderson Land, Esprit and China Life.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, the yen's continued weakness against the U.S. dollar further lifted the country's exporter stocks, a sector that's suffered heavily due to the yen's persistent strength in the past year. The momentum drove the Nikkei to a six month high of 9435.03, as investors took advantage of the yen's fall in response to the Bank of Japan's unexpected move this week to further expand its assets purchase plan and formalize an inflation target.\par \par \uc2 Sony\uc2  rose 1.8% and \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  rose 2.6%.\par \par \uc2 Seoul stocks also rallied as swelling confidence about the U.S. economy raised hopes of a demand-uptick for its big exporters, boosting the market to its highest point since early August last year. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  climbed 3.6%.\par \par \uc2 Earnings results continued to guide individual stock performance in Australia, with \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  rising 1.6% after the country's third-largest bank impressed some analysts with its first quarter profit margin performance. In contrast, earnings downgrades pushed the likes of Brambles and AMP down 0.3%-2.2%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Billabong International\uc2  rocketed 47% to A$2.64 after the Australian surfware retailer rejected a conditional A$3-a-share takeover bid from \uc2 TPG Capital\uc2 , and instead decided to proceed with the partial sale of its boardsports watch unit Nixon Inc.\par \par \uc2 In the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the euro was steady against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The euro's tenure above $1.30 is delicately balanced," said Tim Waterer Sydney-based senior foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at CMC Markets in a note. "It is obvious that Greece and the EU are still not 'singing from the same hymn sheet' when it comes to the austerity plan, and this is adding to the precarious situation of the euro."\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at $1.3130 against the greenback, from $1.3135 late Thursday in New York, and at Y103.81, from Y103.60. The BOJ action continued to hurt the yen as it skirted three and a half month lows against the dollar, with the greenback tapping Y79.13 early Friday, its highest since the Oct. 31 yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was recently at Y79.07, from Y78.94.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar advanced to a six-month high of Y85.49 against the yen, and was recently buying Y85.15, from a low of 84.69 earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,731.10 per troy ounce, up $2.30 from its New York settlement Thursday. March Nymex crude oil futures, which tapped multiweek highs Thursday, added 11 cents to $102.42 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 -Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4142; john.phillips@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 17-02-12 0455GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120217e82h00077\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
