\par\par \b \uc2 Investors Lose Their Yen For Japan's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Shah   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 862 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2012\par 18:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Investors are finally starting to turn against the yen in the options market.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in nearly a decade, hedge funds and other traders appear to be buying more options that profit if the yen weakens against the dollar, as opposed to bets that pay off when the yen strengthens. To a growing number of investors, the yen is losing its appeal as a safe harbor from global economic turmoil. They say Japan's slump, and the increasingly drastic steps the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking to pull the economy out, are becoming too big a drag on the yen at a time when other economies are gathering steam.\par \par \uc2 Wagers against the yen have been a losing proposition for years, and there are still plenty of investors who are skeptical that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has suddenly reversed course. Despite years of slow or negative growth, investors have generally kept buying the yen, viewing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a safe bet thanks to Japan's current-account surplus, which means it doesn't rely on outside creditors, making it seem more fiscally stable. Also, Japanese investors and companies have been repatriating cash from foreign investments, which involves selling other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buying yen, pushing its value higher.\par \par \uc2 The options market is starting to tell a different story. Options grant the right - but not the obligation - to buy or sell a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a preset price, ideal for betting on a trend that may not pan out. One gauge, known as a "risk reversal," stood at minus 0.39 for options that cover the next three months, indicating the strongest preference for bets on a weaker yen since at least October 2003, according to \uc2 Nomura Securities International\uc2 . Since 2009, this gauge has averaged roughly positive 1.7, indicating a strong preference for options that become profitable when the yen's value rises.\par \par \uc2 The gauge first dipped into negative territory on Jan. 23. Longer-term options bets on a weaker yen appear to be gaining popularity too, said Jens Nordvig, global head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Nomura Securities. The "risk reversal" for options covering the next year has dropped to positive 0.04 as of Thursday, indicating a nearly equal preference for positive and negative bets on the yen, following a long period of mostly positive bets.\par \par \uc2 "It's a weak yen story, going forward," said Pierre Lequeux, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management at Aviva Investors in London, which manages roughly GBP270 billion and is owned by British insurer \uc2 Aviva PLC\uc2  (AV, AV.LN).\par \par \uc2 A buyer of yen last year, Lequeux is starting to sell, and instead buying European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the euro and Sweden's krona using forwards-trades he plans to ramp up in coming weeks. Europe's sovereign-debt crisis is easing, he said, while Japan's problems are mounting.\par \par \uc2 The yen traded at Y78.85 to the dollar Thursday, hitting a three-month low for a third consecutive day. On Tuesday, the Bank of Japan surprised markets by expanding a bond-buying program aimed at stimulating the economy, a move that weakened the yen since the bank will be increasing the amount of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year to weaken the yen-whose strength hurts Japanese exporters-and officials have threatened to do so again. On Jan. 25, Japan reported its first annual trade deficit since 1980 last year, as exports suffered in the aftermath of the March 2011 earthquake and nuclear accident. Its economy shrank by an annualized 2.3% in the fourth quarter, a deeper dip than many economists expected.\par \par \uc2 "There is growing evidence that the weak fundamentals of Japan's economy are forcing the Bank of Japan to expand its balance sheet, buying Japanese government bonds as a form of quantitative easing like other developed central banks," said \uc2 Daniel Arbess\uc2 , who manages the $2.1 billion Xerion Fund for New York-based \uc2 Perella Weinberg Partners LP\uc2 . "Monetary debasement is bad for the yen, as it is when done to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." While Arbess would not discuss his positions, he has been negative on the yen in the past.\par \par \uc2 Even if the yen shoots higher, Japanese officials will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength. That makes negative bets on the yen less risky.\par \par \uc2 Tom Liravongsa, co-founder of Michigan-based Holland Global Trading LLC started betting against the yen about two weeks ago, when the exchange rate was Y76.15, closer to levels where \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Having wagered roughly $2.6 million, he eventually exited his position, for a profit, at Y77.60.\par \par \uc2 Ron DiRusso, head of investment research at New York-based \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Concepts LLC, one of the biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 -focused firms, believes some traders in the last several months have bought options to sell the yen in the next year to five years. There is "well over $10 billion [of trades] out there. So that has moved the risk reversal."\par \par \uc2 -By Neil Shah; Wall Street Journal; 212-416-2619; neil.shah@wsj.com [ 02-16-12 1254ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020120216e82g0004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
