\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-BOJ signals more aggressive policy, sets inflation goal\b0\par\par\uc2 966 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2012\par 04:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * BOJ adds 10 trillion yen to asset-buying scheme\par \par \uc2  * BOJ defines 1 pct consumer inflation as price goal\par \par \uc2  * Says to keep zero rates, buy assets until goal foreseen\par \par \uc2  * Comes after \uc2 Fed\uc2  sets inflation target\par \par \uc2  * Bond futures jump, yen dips on surprise move\par \par \uc2  * BOJ Gov promises "massive" bond buying \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, Shirakawa quotes)\par \par \uc2  By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 14 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  boosted its asset buying programme by $130 billion on Tuesday and in the face of political pressure set an inflation goal of 1 percent, signalling a more aggressive monetary policy to pull an ailing economy out of deflation.\par \par \uc2  Bond futures jumped and the yen fell as the decision pointed to much faster asset buying in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most determined effort to date to reinflate an economy that shrank last year and which has struggled with deflation for most of the last two decades.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its policy rate in a range of zero to 0.1 percent and pledged not just to maintain zero rates but to continue buying assets until 1 percent inflation is foreseen.\par \par \uc2  But coming after government calls for action, some analysts worried the policy easing undermined the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's independence and left it open to further pressure from lawmakers, especially if more aggressive U.S. monetary stimulus exerts fresh upward pressure on the yen.\par \par \uc2  "The BOJ apparently bent to political pressure, leaving the market with the impression of its vulnerability," said Yuichi Kodama, an economist at \uc2 Meiji Yasuda Life Insurance\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "If the \uc2 Fed\uc2  embarks on additional easing, the BOJ is likely to come under pressure again and again and it may tweak its asset purchase scheme and start buying government bonds with longer maturities."\par \par \uc2  In a move that surprised markets, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added 10 trillion yen ($130 billion) to its 20 trillion yen pool of funds set aside to buy assets. The new money is earmarked entirely for long-term government bonds.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ said it will set consumer inflation of 1 percent as its price goal for the time being, a clearer commitment to end deflation. It had previously defined 1 percent inflation as its  "understanding" of long-term price stability.\par \par \uc2  "We will be buying massive amounts of government bonds," Governor \uc2 Masaaki Shirakawa\uc2  told reporters. "This is designed to achieve sustained economic growth under stable prices."\par \par \uc2  Ten-year government bond futures jumped and the yen\par \par \uc2 dipped as much as 0.4 percent on the BOJ news.\par \par \uc2  TIMING PUZZLES MARKETS\par \par \uc2  Most central banks set inflation targets to anchor expectations and prevent prices from rising too quickly. In Japan's case, an inflation goal will act as an incentive to pursue more aggressive easing to stop prices from declining.\par \par \uc2  Core inflation fell in 2011 for the third straight year.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2  last month took a historic step of setting an  inflation target and extended its commitment to near zero rates, leaving the door open to more easing that could further weaken the dollar and put the yen under renewed upward pressure.\par \par \uc2  The yen rose to record highs against the dollar last year, sparking a wave of corporate calls for official action and prompting unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Oct 31.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ's steps follow weeks of growing calls from politicians to follow the \uc2 Fed\uc2 's example and set an explicit inflation target to show a greater commitment to boosting the economy, which shrank 0.9 percent last year largely as yen strength hurt export earnings.\par \par \uc2  The timing came as a surprise as many central bankers had suggested there was little reason to act now because the yen had backed off its record highs and the economy was on course for a moderate recovery.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ eased policy while maintaining expectations for a moderate recovery, unlike in the past when it responded to a worsening economic outlook. Shirakawa said Tuesday's decision was aimed at giving the recovery additional support, shrugging off suggestions the BOJ had yielded to government pressure or was monetising debt.\par \par \uc2  "There are some bright economic signs at home and abroad. We'd like to ensure they are sustained," he said.\par \par \uc2  MORE TO COME\par \par \uc2  While the BOJ changed its language in describing 1 percent consumer inflation as a near-term price "goal," it avoided describing it as an explicit target.\par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Jun Azumi, however, told reporters he considered 1 percent as an effective inflation target, a sign the government will keep up pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take further action.\par \par \uc2  Even under the new 30-trillion-yen target, the BOJ needs to buy 20 trillion yen more assets by year-end under time limits set in the scheme. It would mean buying government bonds at triple the pace from now.\par \par \uc2  In addition to the asset buying scheme, the BOJ has an existing 35 trillion yen pool of funds for lending to banks against collateral at the policy rate.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ may act again in coming months and is quietly reviewing an alternative to its asset buying scheme, which can probably only be topped up once or twice given it is already struggling to feed cash to markets awash with extra liquidity.\par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has decided to buy quite a large amount of JGBs and its mention of a stability goal with a specific target was not expected until the spring. It has acted more strongly than expected," said Katsutoshi Inadome, fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\par \par \uc2  "The bank still has further easing options left, like increasing the amount of assets it buys and buying JGBs with longer maturities."\par \par \uc2 ($1=77.64 yen) \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Rie Ishiguro, Kaori Kaneko and Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (UPDATE 3)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120217e82e0005s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
