\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Little Changed In Volatile Session\b0\par\par\uc2 2,445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2012\par 23:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12878.28  +4.24  +0.03% \par Nasdaq     2931.83  +0.44  +0.02% \par S&P 500    1350.50  -1.27  -0.09% \par FTSE 100   5899.87  -5.83  -0.10% \par Xetra DAX  6728.19 -10.28  -0.15% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 29/32  flat  0.290% flat \par 5Y Tsy  100 10/32  +4/32 0.812% -2.8BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 21/32 +18/32 1.928% -6.2BP \par  \par USD/JPY    78.44-46  +1.14% \par Range      78.55-77.38 \par EUR/USD    1.3114-16  -0.54% \par Range      1.3215-1.3080 \par AUD/USD    1.0672-76  -0.54% \par Range      1.0738-1.0630 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    8.25/7.35% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   11.35/10.40% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   12.00/11.75% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,717.70    -$7.20 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,713.80-$1,729.90 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $100.74    -$0.17 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks ended little changed Tuesday, after a volatile session in which the market flirted with its worst point decline of the year, only to recover in the last half hour of trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average was down as much as 87 points mid-afternoon, but had recovered by the close to end 4.2 points higher at 12878. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index finished down 1.3 points, or 0.1%, at 1351, and the Nasdaq Composite closed up 0.4 point at 2932.\par \par \uc2 Half of the S&P's 10 sectors finished higher, led by consumer staples, while half finished lower, dragged down by materials. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  lost the most among blue chips, down 3.3%, while \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  rose the most, up 1.1%.\par \par \uc2 Stocks spent nearly all day in negative territory, taking their cue from disappointing U.S. retail sales data and continued frustrations with euro-zone debt resolution.\par \par \uc2 Retail and food-service sales rose 0.4% last month, below an increase of 0.9% anticipated by economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Major indexes extended losses in the afternoon after euro-zone finance ministers postponed a meeting to discuss Greece's second bailout.\par \par \uc2 "The economic numbers were not all that robust, and that tempered things. More negative news just adds to the market going south," said Stephen Carl, head equity trader at Williams Capital Group.\par \par \uc2 Michael Kors Holdings\uc2  rose 27% after it beat fiscal third-quarter earnings and revenue estimates, and provided an upbeat revenue outlook for the current quarter.\par \par \uc2 Goodyear Tire & Rubber\uc2  dropped 5.2% after it reported disappointing fourth-quarter results, and said it expects near-term growth in the global tire industry at a slower pace than previously expected, given continued economic weakness in multiple markets.\par \par \uc2 Asian bourses were mixed Tuesday. China's Shanghai Composite edged lower, weighed down by sovereign-debt downgrades in Europe, while Japan's Nikkei Stock Average rose after the Bank of Japan eased monetary policy by expanding its asset purchase program.\par \par \uc2 "The (China) market may consolidate with a downward bias in the coming sessions as heavyweight banks and property plays lack strong upward momentum after recent gains," said Gold State Securities analyst Huang Bin. The National Social Security Fund's decision to sell shares in two lenders may indicate the banking sector needs to take a breather after recent rallies, he added.\par \par \uc2 Looking further ahead, analysts said the Shanghai index could rebound to 2500 in the coming weeks on hopes for fourth-quarter earnings growth for listed companies, which are scheduled to issue their results next month.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended slightly higher Tuesday, as strength in property stocks there more than offset the negative impact from \uc2 Moody's\uc2  decision to cut ratings or outlooks for several European countries.\par \par \uc2 "Some investors are awaiting key corporate earnings from major banks and developers in the coming weeks for more trading cues," Linus Yip, a strategist at First Shanghai Securities, said, adding that investors will likely focus on key economic data from China to gauge that country's economic health and monetary policy stance.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks closed at their highest level in nearly six months after the BoJ's announcement it would expand its asset purchase program fueled a rise in real estate and marine transport shares.\par \par \uc2 "All in all, today's decision was a welcome surprise and we expect the decision to marginally depress long term interest rates and the yen," said Takuji Okubo, Chief Japan Economist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  Corporate & Investment Banking. "But the element of surprise was too small to make a big difference on the market's perception on the BOJ's inability to reflate the economy."\par \par \uc2 Nymex crude oil futures prices settled slightly lower Tuesday amid conflicting economic signals from Europe.\par \par \uc2 Comex gold futures also ended a touch lower Tuesday, amid pressure from a stronger dollar and caution about an increasingly unstable European debt situation.\par \par \uc2 Base metal prices ended Tuesday lower after U.S. retail sales figures disappointed, offsetting the release of upbeat German sentiment data earlier in the global day.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen plunged to its lowest level in more than three months against the dollar Tuesday after the Bank of Japan unexpectedly expanded its asset purchase program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  caught markets off guard by ramping up its bond-buying by an additional Y10 trillion, and set a de-facto inflation target in an effort to pull the country out of its deflationary spiral. The asset purchases undermine the yen by increasing the money supply, much like the bond buying undertaken in 2010 by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  weakened the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the yen already under pressure because of Japan's grim economic fundamentals, the news encouraged traders to snap up dollars. The greenback shot up more than 1%, with its session high of Y78.54 marking the yen's weakest point since Nov. 1, the day after the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  launched a massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to knock the yen back from post-World War II highs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen ended at Y78.45 to the dollar, down 1.1% from Y77.57 late Monday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts, however, predicted that the yen would soon begin to edge higher again, especially because the world's largest economies are embarking on what \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Tuesday called "competitive quantitative easing." Speculation is rampant that the Fed may soon unleash more quantitative easing, or QE3.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think in the long-run (a weak yen is) going to be sustainable, with the prospect of QE3 still on the table," said Chris Fernandes, vice president and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  advisor at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of the West\f28 \uc2 \f2 , based in San Francisco.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because Greece's debt fears are still sending traders in search of safe-harbor \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , "I foresee risk-aversion rearing its ugly head again," Fernandes said, which will drive investors back to the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At least for one day, the news out of Japan momentarily diverted traders' attention away from Greece. Fears that Greece may default are festering, despite parliamentary backing of key austerity measures designed to secure a tranche of international assistance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro came under renewed selling pressure after euro-zone finance ministers scrapped a planned meeting in Brussels Wednesday that had been called to approve Greece's bailout and debt restructuring.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "After the weekend's vote, people are realizing there's more stuff to be done, and the stuff that needs to be done is being postponed," said Brian Kim, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at RBS Securities. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is suffering from "a post-Greek vote hangover."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Tuesday, the euro was at $1.3130 from $1.3186 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. Against the yen, the euro was at Y103.03 from Y102.36. The U.K. pound was at $1.5680 from $1.5770. The dollar was at CHF0.9193 from CHF0.9165.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 79.388 from 78.946.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury bonds drew investors in Tuesday, as uncertainty over the euro zone's debt crisis and disappointing U.S. retail sales boosted demand for safe assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A day after Greece approved austerity measures, a precondition for a bond-swap deal with private investors to avoid a default next month, a setback jolted investors Tuesday as the euro-zone finance ministers meeting scheduled for Wednesday was called off. It was replaced with a teleconference as Greek political leaders haven't given clear pledges on the implementation of the belt-tightening measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It was a reminder that we are not yet out of the woods," said Sean Simko, who manages a $6.5 billion fixed-income portfolio at SEI Investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, a report showing U.S. retail sales gained a smaller-than-forecast 0.4% last month supported a view in the markets that the world's largest economy may still need further stimulus from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 --even though some analysts argue the only notable soft spot from the report was a decline in auto sales.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy makers still left the door open for a fresh bond-buying program should the economic recovery, which in recent weeks had showed signs of improvement, lose steam again.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Bond bulls are trying to make a case that the economy will lose momentum in the first quarter just like last year, so any weaker-than-expected number gives them hope," said Thomas Roth, executive director in the U.S. government bond trading group at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities (USA) Inc. in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late afternoon trade, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was 18/32 higher to yield 1.928%. The 30-year bond was 1 10/32 higher to yield 3.072%. The two-year note was flat to yield 0.290%. Bond prices move inversely to their yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended flat Tuesday as a steady monetary policy outlook offset concerns about a potential short-term liquidity squeeze caused by a large initial public offering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In addition, expectations of easing inflationary pressures this month are also keeping bond prices steady," a Shanghai-based trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The index may face slight downside pressure in the next two days as China Communications Construction's IPO opens for subscriptions from Tuesday to Wednesday, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Communications Construction said Tuesday that it had set an indicative range of CNY5.00-CNY5.40 a share for its planned initial public offering in Shanghai. [ 14-02-12 2230GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Based on that range, the company would raise between CNY8.00 billion and CNY8.64 billion from the sale. That's above the CNY5 billion the company said late last month it was aiming to raise in the IPO after cutting the number of shares it planned to offer. It had originally targeted proceeds of CNY20 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond futures rose Tuesday after the Bank of Japan's unexpected credit easing sent investors scrambling to buy back safe but low-yielding bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The announcement was "a positive surprise" for the market, said Naomi Hasegawa, senior strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ said that it will increase the amount of JGBs it purchases by Y10 trillion and complete the purchases by the end of 2012. Bank of Japan Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Masaaki Shirakawa\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday's easing would raise JGB buying to Y1.5 trillion a month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Wednesday, Malaysian economic growth is likely to have slowed to 4.8% in the fourth quarter from 5.8% in the third quarter, the median forecast of 11 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires shows. For the full year, the economy is expected to have grown 5.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yahoo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Asia Deal Talks Hit Snag \par Eurogroup To Delay Meeting On Greece \par US Retail Sales Climb; Import Prices Stay In Check \par Greece Has Resolved Outstanding Issues With Eurogroup - Govt Official \par Iran Sees Consensus On Banning Oil Exports To EU \par Obama, Xi Get A First Look At Each Other \par Geithner Sees Corporate Tax Framework 'Soon' \par Irish Fin Min Says Economy To Continue To Grow \par US Gasoline Use Drops Again In Latest Week \par Westfield Enters US$4.8 Bln Venture With Canada Pension \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zynga\f28 \uc2 \f2  Posts 4Q Loss On Compensation Costs \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Metlife\f28 \uc2 \f2  4Q Net Surges On International Growth \par Apple Testing Smaller IPad Tablet With Suppliers In Asia \par S Korea Import Price Rise Accelerates In January On Costlier Oil \par New Zealand 4Q Real Retail Sales +2.2% On Quarter, Beat Expectations \par Tax-Deferred Deal Between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yahoo\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Asian Partners Off the Table -- Sources \par Xi Urges 'Greater Balance' In US-China Trade, Investment \par Toyota CFO Confident Of Earnings Recovery In FY12 -Nikkei \par Aeon Bank To Absorb Former Incubator Bank In March -Nikkei \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2300  S Korea      Jan Economically Active Population Survey, incl \par                    Unemployment \par 2330  Australia    Feb \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  - Melbourne Institute Consumer Sentiment \par                    Survey \par 0001  UK           Jan Scottish Retail Sales Monitor \par 0030  Australia    Dec Lending Finance \par 0030  Australia    Jan New Motor Vehicles Sales \par 0200  New Zealand  Jan NZ Government bonds held for non-residents \par 0300  Australia    Feb DEEWR Monthly Leading Indicator of Employment \par 0300  S Korea      Jan Revised trade data \par 0400  Malaysia     4Q  Balance of Payments \par 0500  Japan        Feb Bank of Japan Monthly Report \par 0500  Singapore    Dec Retail Sales \par 0630  France       4Q  GDP - preliminary figures \par 0700  Germany      4Q  GDP - 1st release \par 0800  Hong Kong    Jan Hong Kong port container throughput monthly \par                    estimates \par 0930  UK           Jan UK monthly unemployment figures \par 1000  Malaysia     4Q  GDP \par 1000  EU           4Q  Flash Estimate GDP \par 1000  EU           Dec Foreign trade \par 1030  UK           Bank of England inflation report \par 1200  US           Feb 10 MBA Weekly Mortgage Applications Survey \par 1330  US           Feb Empire State Manufacturing Survey \par 1400  US   