\par\par \b \uc2 DJ WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Yen Slumps To 3-Month Low After BoJ Ramps Up Bond Purchases\b0\par\par\uc2 By Javier E. David   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2012\par 23:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--The yen plunged to its lowest level in more than three months against the dollar Tuesday after the Bank of Japan unexpectedly expanded its asset purchase program.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught markets off guard by ramping up its bond-buying by an additional Y10 trillion, and set a de-facto inflation target in an effort to pull the country out of its deflationary spiral. The asset purchases undermine the yen by increasing the money supply, much like the bond buying undertaken in 2010 by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  weakened the dollar.\par \par \uc2 With the yen already under pressure because of Japan's grim economic fundamentals, the news encouraged traders to snap up dollars. The greenback shot up by more than 1%, with its session high of Y78.54 marking its weakest point since Nov. 1, the day after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched a massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to knock the yen back from post-World War II highs.\par \par \uc2 The yen ended at Y78.45 to the dollar, down 1.1% from Y77.57 late Monday in New York.\par \par \uc2 Analysts, however, predicted that the yen would soon begin to edge higher again, especially because the world's largest economies are embarking on what \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  on Tuesday called "competitive quantitative easing." Speculation is rampant that the Fed may soon unleash more quantitative easing, or QE3.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think in the long-run (a weak yen is) going to be sustainable, with the prospect of QE3 still on the table," said Chris Fernandes, vice president and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor at \uc2 Bank of the West\uc2 , based in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Because Greece's debt fears are still sending traders in search of safe-harbor \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "I foresee risk-aversion rearing its ugly head again," Fernandes said, which will drive investors back to the yen.\par \par \uc2 The BoJ's decision was the latest in a series of novel moves demonstrating Japan's resolve to combat a strong yen, which is undercutting the country's efforts to engineer an economic recovery. In the final quarter of 2011, the world's third largest economy shrank at an annualized 2.3%, which coincided with exports tumbling to their weakest level in more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 In August, Japan set up a $100 billion facility to encourage companies to exchange yen for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  disclosed earlier this month that it had secretly sold yen during the first week of November, in the immediate wake of its Oct. 31 yen-selling spree.\par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday, the euro was at $1.3130 from $1.3186 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. Against the yen, the euro was at Y103.03 from Y102.36. The U.K. pound was at $1.5680 from $1.5770. The dollar was at CHF0.9193 from CHF0.9165.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 79.388 from about 78.946.\par \par \uc2 At least for one day, the news out of Japan momentarily diverted traders' attention away from Greece. Fears that the Hellenic republic may default are festering, despite parliamentary backing of key austerity measures designed to secure a tranche of international assistance.\par \par \uc2 The euro came under renewed selling pressure after euro zone finance ministers scrapped a planned meeting in Brussels Wednesday that had been called to approve Greece's bailout and debt restructuring. Athens' warring political factions have failed to give European leaders clear pledges on how to implement new belt-tightening moves the country's parliament approved on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 "After the weekend's vote, people are realizing there's more stuff to be done, and the stuff that needs to be done is being postponed," said Brian Kim, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS Securities. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suffering from "a post-Greek vote hangover."\par \par \uc2 -By Javier E. David, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4564; javier.david@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120214e82e00bhx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
