\par\par \b \uc2 DJ GLOBAL CALL: Japanese Finance Minister Is Every Yen Options Trader's Best Friend\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Baskin  \par \uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2012\par 15:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 1. Japanese Finance Minister Jun Azumi became every yen options trader's best friend when in an unusual, and no doubt instantly regretted bit of candor he told lawmakers exactly where his government looked to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For the record, Azumi said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in when the yen hits Y75.63 and stopped at Y78.20. Those figures are the closing dollar-yen rate on Oct. 30, before the Y8 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and on Nov. 5, the day after the last of the smaller "stealth" \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that followed the big one. Also for the record, Azumi later said he "didn't talk about any levels at all. Period." At Y77.64 to the dollar, the yen was too far from either target on Friday for Azumi's comments to have an immediate impact. But \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  surely wasn't the only bank to rearrange its approach to the yen. "We look to re-establish USD/JPY shorts at better levels.\par \par \uc2  We aim to enter at 78.20, the level that Finance Minister Azumi stopped USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ...\par \par \uc2 our target is 75.70, just above the 75.63 level Azumi cited for starting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the bank said.\par \par \uc2 2. Continuing the theme of finance ministers seemingly unaware that everyone can hear them when they talk: German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble told his Portuguese counterpart on the sidelines of a conference that Portugal's bailout could be adjusted once Greece is sorted out. Once those comments hit \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , they became the first instance of a senior European official publicly indicating that Portugal's bailout terms could change. While not a total surprise, the comments are evidence that Europe's recovery only begins in Greece; Portugal, and perhaps Ireland, will require new solutions as well. So much for ECB Chairman Mario Draghi's comments on Thursday that Greece is "unique".\par \par \uc2 3. The long-awaited Greek debt agreement hasn't given markets much of a bounce. Euro-zone finance ministers said they'll wait for Greece to actually pass its promised austerity measures before signing off on aid. After so many false starts over the last year, markets aren't moving until the money starts flowing.\par \par \uc2 4. China's financial institutions issued CNY738.1 billion in new loans in January, less than expected, as China continues to hold off on opening the floodgates of monetary stimulus. Inflation remains the primary concern for now, though the 47% year-on-year drop in loans, as well as a big jump in the country's trade surplus, is a worrisome indicator for future growth, and points to more easing in the future.\par \par \uc2 5. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  cut its near-term growth and inflation outlooks and said it still had room to cut interest rates. This comes after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly failed to cut rates earlier this week. Much will hinge on Europe, and the extent to which the continent's debt crisis weighs on the global economy.\par \par \uc2 6. Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tweaked its plan to encourage growth by halting interest payments on reserves deposited by large banks. The payments won't stop until August, from April, and will resume in 2014. By withholding interest, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hopes to encourage large banks to buy loan portfolios from smaller banks, which in turn will have a new incentive to lend, boosting economic growth. The only hitch will be if the stimulus works too well and bumps up inflation.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Baskin at brian.baskin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 10, 2012 09:16 ET (14:16 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120210e82a004fz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
