\par\par \b \uc2 Japan lawmaker urges use of pension fund to curb yen\b0\par\par\uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2012\par 04:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 9 (Reuters) - Japan's public pension fund, the world's largest, should shift more of its investments into foreign assets from domestic bonds to help weaken the yen, a senior ruling party official said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2  "It would have the same effect as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Seiji Maehara, the Democratic Party of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy chief, told a parliament session.\par \par \uc2  Japanese policy-makers worry that the yen's strength damages the economy by making exports more expensive.\par \par \uc2  The Government Pension Investment Fund (GPIF) had assets of 108.9 trillion yen ($1.42 trillion) at the end of September, nearly as big as the Russian economy. About 17 percent was in foreign assets.\par \par \uc2  Maehara said the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  could prevent a shift of funds by the GPIF from triggering a spike in long-term interest rates by boosting its purchases of Japanese government bonds.\par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda was noncommittal, saying the idea could expose pension funds to too much \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk and complicate relations with other countries if it aims for an effect similar to yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  But he said the idea was worth looking into. "We need to set a certain direction (on this idea) after discussions looking at various aspects," he said.\par \par \uc2  The idea may not make much headway soon given that the GPIF suffered huge losses on foreign bonds and equities in the July-September quarter due to the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  But the proposal underscores the growing frustration among lawmakers over the stubbornly strong yen, which neared its record high of 75.31 yen against the dollar last week despite verbal threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the finance minister. On Thursday it was trading around 77.15 yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent roughly 9 trillion yen in unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  late last year last year to prevent a yen spike from derailing a fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2  Tokyo has signalled its readiness to step in again to counter speculative moves, although U.S. criticism over its past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  has led to market views it may be hesitant to act again. That has led to growing calls from the government and lawmakers for the BOJ to loosen monetary policy further.\par \par \uc2  Maehara said monetary easing was an important tool for Japan to overcome deflation. Noda was more restrained in his tone, saying the government and the BOJ should work closely together to support the economy.\par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor \uc2 Masaaki Shirakawa\uc2 , attending the same parliamentary committee, offered few clues on whether the BOJ will ease again soon, only saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to do its utmost to pull Japan out of deflation.\par \par \uc2  "Japan's economy is in a severe state. We fully understand that pulling Japan out of deflation as soon as possible, and achieving sustainable economic growth with price stability, are very important goals," Shirakawa said.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ is inclined to stand pat at its policy meeting next week, but may ponder easing further or clarify its commitment to beating deflation in the face of growing calls for more steps to support the economy. \par \par \uc2  Maehara is a veteran ruling party lawmaker in charge of conveying to the government the party's stance on key policies.\par \par \uc2  His calls for bolder steps to counter a strong yen in the past nudged the government into boosting a programme offering credit lines to banks that provide loans to Japanese firms pursuing overseas corporate buyouts. \par \par \uc2 ($1 = 76.8950 Japanese yen) \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/YEN-MAEHARA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120212e82900029\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
