\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-BOJ FOCUS-Wary of Fed-driven yen spike, BOJ poised to act\b0\par\par\uc2 1,009 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2012\par 01:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Repeats item from Friday with no changes to text)\par \par \uc2  * BOJ in no mood to ease now but always ready to act\par \par \uc2  * Some fret of Fed-driven yen rise hitting weak economy\par \par \uc2  * Tokyo won't hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , holding breath for now\par \par \uc2  * Policymakers determined to avoid repeat of 2010 \par \par \uc2  By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 3 (Reuters) - When the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  flagged a new round of aggressive monetary easing back in August 2010, the yen rally that followed wrong-footed Japanese central bankers and forced them to act under pressure from angry politicians and fast-moving markets.\par \par \uc2  Today, as the yen climbs again on expectations of more \uc2 Fed\uc2  quantitative easing -- injecting vast amounts of cash into the economy via government bond purchases -- the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is determined to avoid making the same mistake again.\par \par \uc2  For now, Tokyo still relies on verbal threats to keep traders at bay, but the BOJ has its finger on the trigger and is ready to act once the yen's climb gains momentum and threatens the economy's fragile recovery from the March 11 disaster.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2 's pledge to keep interest rates near zero at least until late 2014 has spurred speculation that another round of hefty Treasury bond buying will follow and Japanese policymakers are now trying to anticipate how markets will respond.\par \par \uc2  Back in 2010 Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke's hint at more bond buying in a speech in August and expectations of hundreds of billions of dollars flowing into the market accelerated the yen's rise, pushing it to 15-year highs against the dollar in September.\par \par \uc2  Japanese officials were surprised by the extent of yen gains and the BOJ had to call an emergency meeting on Aug. 30 just three weeks after a regular rate review.\par \par \uc2  "We can't let the same thing happen again," said a source familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking.\par \par \uc2  Any easing in response to sharp or prolonged yen rises would most likely come through a further increase in asset purchases.\par \par \uc2  "Our basic stance is to always be ready to act. This is particularly important when other major central banks are all in easing mode," said another source. Both sources spoke on condition of anonymity due to the sensitivity of the matter.\par \par \uc2  Tokyo also will not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen rises and carefully wait for the best timing to maximize the effect. Finance Minister Jun Azumi on Friday turned up the heat, describing recent yen moves as speculative and threatening to act decisively against "one-sided" moves. \par \par \uc2  Many market players say the trigger could be a sharp yen spike past the record 75.32 to the dollar hit last October.\par \par \uc2  "Authorities probably won't leave unattended a dollar fall below 76 or 75. If they were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it could be when the dollar falls below 75 because once that level is breached, yen rises may accelerate," said Yunosuke Ikeda, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Nomura Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2  Even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds off on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to G7 criticism over last year's solo actions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may ease policy in coming months if yen rises are sustained long enough to pose severe damage to business sentiment, analysts say.\par \par \uc2  BOJ Deputy Governor \uc2 Hirohide Yamaguchi\uc2  on Thursday ruled out the chance of immediate easing but said the bank is mindful of the strong uncertainty on the outlook, signalling its readiness to offer further monetary stimulus if necessary.\par \par \uc2  BOLDER STEPS?\par \par \uc2  Back in 2010, Japan's economy was performing well in the first half, expanding an annualised 6.5 percent in the first quarter and 4.6 percent in the next quarter, supported by solid exports to fast-growing emerging economies.\par \par \uc2  That led the BOJ to underestimate the pain from yen gains. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  began to rise in June and climbed 7 percent by the end of July. It took another 3 percent rise in August, which triggered steep falls in Tokyo stocks, for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease policy.\par \par \uc2  The finance ministry was also slow in following up, finally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen in mid-September after the BOJ's August move failed to halt the yen's ascent.\par \par \uc2  The cost of slow and poorly coordinated actions were all too clear with growth slowing to 2 percent in July-September and grinding to a halt in the following quarter.\par \par \uc2  Today the government and the BOJ coordinate better. They \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and eased policy simultaneously in March, August and October last year.\par \par \uc2  The economy is also now more vulnerable to external shocks, warranting a quicker policy response.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ is counting on fiscal spending for reconstruction from the March earthquake to offset some of the pain from slowing overseas demand and the yen's rises. But it now says a pickup in activity initially expected early this year may get delayed for several months.\par \par \uc2  For now, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hopes to counter any adverse market moves by topping up its 55 trillion yen ($722 billion) asset buying fund, under which it purchases government and private debt.\par \par \uc2  It is unlikely to revert to full-scale quantitative easing of 2001-2006, when it targeted deposits parked at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it believes it had little effect in stimulating the economy.\par \par \uc2  But with further increases in the asset-buying fund largely priced in, the BOJ may be under pressure to do more -- such as buying more longer-term government bonds and private assets.\par \par \uc2  "If the government feels it's hard to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market given U.S. opposition, the BOJ will have to ease policy regardless of whether it is effective or not," said Koichi Haji, chief economist at NLI Research Institute In Tokyo.\par \par \uc2  "The BOJ doesn't want to revert to old-style QE but then, it would have to come up with a different extraordinary move."\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 76.1500 Japanese yen) \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Kaori Kaneko and Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (ANALYSIS) (REPEAT)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120209e8260000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
