\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Rise Amid US Job Additions\b0\par\par\uc2 2,016 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2012\par 23:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA               12862.23    +156.82    +1.23 % \par Nasdaq              2905.66     +45.98    +1.61 % \par S&P 500             1344.90     +19.36    +1.46 % \par FTSE 100            5901.07    +105.00    +1.81 % \par Xetra DAX           6766.67    +111.04    +1.67 % \par  \par 2Y Tsy   100 1/32      -1/32  0.238%    +1.2 \par 5Y Tsy   100 15/32     -10/32 0.777%    +6.6 \par 10Y Tsy  100 19/32     -30/32 1.933%   +10.7 \par  \par USD/JPY Japan         76.21-24  +0.45% \par Range                 76.73 - 76.15 \par EUR/USD Euro        1.3142-45  +0.02% \par Range                 1.3205 - 1.3068 \par AUD/USD Australia   1.0710-13  +0.56% \par Range                 1.0794-1.0674 \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,140.30   -$19.00 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,728.40-$1,765.90 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $97.84      +$1.48 \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 Stocks rose after the U.S. economy added more jobs than expected last month, driving the Nasdaq Composite to an 11-year high and pushing the Dow to its highest close in nearly four years.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average advanced 156.82 points, or 1.2%, to 12862.23, ending the week with its best finish since May 19, 2008. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index tacked on 19.36 points, or 1.5%, to 1344.90, capping off a fifth-straight weekly gain.\par \par \uc2 The technology-oriented Nasdaq Composite Index gained 45.98 points, or 1.6%, to 2905.66, closing at its highest level since December 2000.\par \par \uc2 All three major indexes are considered officially in a bull market as of Friday's market action, defined as 20% or higher above October's closing lows. The S&P 500, up 6.9% so far this year, and the Nasdaq, up 12%, are each off to their best starts to a year in over two decades.\par \par \uc2 All 10 of the S&P 500's sectors rose Friday, with financials and consumer-discretionary stocks leading the way. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  rose 39 cents, or 5.2%, to $7.84, while Caterpillar added $3.61, or 3.3%, to $113.94.\par \par \uc2 Friday's gains followed a strong report from the U.S. Labor Department. January data showed nonfarm payrolls rose 243,000 last month, marking the biggest gain since April. The jobless rate fell from 8.5% to 8.3%, the lowest it has been since February 2009.\par \par \uc2 Stocks popped higher at the open and then cruised with its gains through the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 "The data seems to show the economy is mending a bit faster than it was a year, or two years ago," said David Resler, chief economic adviser for Nomura Securities.\par \par \uc2 Other data showed that the U.S. nonmanufacturing sector expanded at a faster rate in January, while a factory orders came in lower than expectations.\par \par \uc2 European markets also rose on encouraging economic data. The Stoxx Europe 600 gained 1.7% and closed at a six-month high. The euro-zone composite purchasing manager's index confirmed private-sector activity expanded in January, with the index rising to 50.4 from 48.3 in December.\par \par \uc2 Asian bourses were mixed, with Japan's Nikkei Stock Average down 0.5% and China's Shanghai Composite up 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures dropped 1.1%, to $1737.90 a troy ounce, while crude-oil prices rose 1.5% to $97.84 a barrel. The yield on the 10-year Treasury rose to 1.943%.\par \par \uc2 In corporate news, \uc2 Tyson Foods\uc2  rallied 76 cents, or 4.1%, to 19.38, after the meat processor reported fiscal first-quarter earnings and revenue that topped expectations, boosted by a return to profitability in its chicken business.\par \par \uc2 Genworth Financial\uc2  swung to a fourth-quarter profit as the insurer enjoyed net investment gains rather than losses and reported lower policy benefits. Shares climbed 1.13, or 14%, to 9.17.\par \par \uc2 Gilead Sciences\uc2 ' fourth-quarter earnings rose 5.7% as the biopharmaceutical company's product sales continued to rise and it no longer booked deep reductions in royalties. Shares gained 5.38, or 11%, to 54.70.\par \par \uc2 Edwards Lifesciences\uc2  fell 9.13, or 11%, 71.54 after the company reported fourth-quarter earnings that fell 2.6% on higher charges and issued muted projections for this quarter.\par \par \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2 ' fourth-quarter profit climbed 67% on a tax benefit and as the casino operator's Macau \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to post revenue growth, yet a battle between Chief Executive \uc2 Steve Wynn\uc2  and a shareholder overshadowed the results. Shares fell 5.80, or 4.8%, to 114.98.\par \par \uc2 THQ tumbled 23 cents, or 30%, to 53 cents, after the interactive-games maker reported fiscal third-quarter earnings that were well below expectations, while revenue was in line with its previously lowered outlook.\par \par \uc2 Trimble Navigation\uc2  leapt 2.69, or 5.6%, to 50.83 after the company reported fourth-quarter earnings and revenue that exceeded forecasts and provided a first-quarter outlook that was above projections.\par \par \uc2 Estee Lauder\uc2  shed 1.37, or 2.3%, to 57.48 after the skin-care and fragrance-products maker's fiscal second-quarter results matched estimates, but its third-quarter earnings outlook was below projections.\par \par \uc2 Acme Packet\uc2  rose 2.80, or 9.1%, to 33.69, though the networking equipment maker's fourth-quarter earnings and revenue came in below previously lowered expectations.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 A surprisingly strong jobs report boosted the dollar Friday, as evidence of a rebounding labor market helped diminish expectations of more expansionary monetary policy by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With markets still being roiled by Greece's efforts to restructure its debt in order to receive fresh financial assistance, investors cheered news that the U.S. economy added 243,000 jobs last month. The jobless rate fell to 8.3%, its lowest since February 2009.\par \par \uc2 Since last week's Fed policy meeting, in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledged to keep its key interest rate at near zero through late 2014, investors have been reluctant to hold dollars. Analysts said that a strengthening labor market may alleviate one of the Fed's major concerns about the economy, thus curtailing the likelihood of a new round of potential bond buying that tends to weaken the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The jobs report "forced traders to adjust their expectations for" a third round of quantitative easing, said Kathy Lien, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York. "We went from pricing in stimulus to pricing in a rate hike as early as 2014."\par \par \uc2 The strong data works at cross purposes. Although it helped staunch concerns amongst \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders about QE3, the numbers bolstered the market's appetite for stocks and higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That helped the euro reverse the day's losses despite ongoing jitters about Greece's ability to strike a long-awaited deal with its private bondholders.\par \par \uc2 Going forward, strong data "will be more positive for the euro than the dollar," Lien said, because euro-zone yields are currently higher than the rock-bottom returns offered in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday, the euro was at $1.3163 from $1.3144 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.64 from Y76.21, while the euro was at Y100.83 from Y100.20. The U.K. pound was at $1.5822 from $1.5807. The dollar was at CHF0.9190 from CHF0.9168, while the euro was at CHF1.2080 from CHF1.2050.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 78.939 from about 78.991.\par \par \uc2 One of the day's biggest movers was the yen, which weakened broadly on concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. Over the last several days, the dollar has steadily closed in on last year's record low at Y75.31, prompting Japanese officials to warn that they would take "decisive measures" to counteract the safe-haven buying that normally drives the yen higher.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 Uplifting U.S. economic releases on Friday drove investors out of safe-harbor Treasury bonds, fueling the biggest one-day selloff for the benchmark 10-year note since late October.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year note's yield, a key rate for the U.S. government to borrow in capital markets, rose by 11 basis points for the session and hit a one-week peak of 1.956%. Still, the yield remains low--less than 30 basis points from a historic trough set in September.\par \par \uc2 German bunds and U.K. gilts also took a beating. The selloff in all these three major government bond markets was a balm for investors whose sentiments have been whipped around by the uncertainty over the euro zone's sovereign debt crisis and its potential impact on the global economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 Boosting investors' mood Friday, the U.S. added 243,000 non-farm jobs last month, the fastest pace of expansion since April. The unemployment rate unexpectedly dipped to 8.3%, the lowest in three years, though the level remains historically high. Meanwhile, a key gauge of the U.S. service sector posted a bigger increase than economists forecast.\par \par \uc2 The twin reports dented some speculation of a new round of bond-buying by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to stimulate the economy in coming months. As a result, long-dated Treasurys, which have benefited the most from the Fed's purchases since October, were hit the hardest, especially as both the 10-year and 30-year bonds face new supply in the coming week.\par \par \uc2 "The risk-on mentality is back on," said Sean Simko, who manages a $6.5 billion fixed-income portfolio at SEI Investments. "The data helps support U.S. growth prospects. This data doesn't rule out but does buy the Fed more time to assess the economic landscape and the options of an additional stimulus package."\par \par \uc2 In late-afternoon trading, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note fell by 30/32 in price, pushing up the yield to 1.933%. The yield rose from 1.896% at the end of last week, and the weekly price loss reversed the rally in the prior week. Bond prices move inversely to their yields.\par \par \uc2 The 30-year bond was 2 12/32 lower to yield 3.135%.\par \par \uc2 The two-year note was relatively unscathed with its price down 1/32 to yield 0.238%. Its yield is well anchored by the Fed's near-zero key policy rate. [ 03-02-12 2248GMT ]\par \par \uc2 The Fed last week extended the ultra-low rate policy through late 2014 but has said that further monetary stimulus are data-dependent, a stance reiterated by Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Stocks Rise On Strong Jobs Data \par Canada Outlines New Oil-Sands Environmental Monitoring System \par US Payrolls Jump 243,000; Jobless Rate Falls To 8.3% \par Micron CEO Dies In Plane Crash \par BofA Mortgage Exec \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barbara Desoer\f28 \uc2 \f2  Is Leaving \par Greece Says Creditor Talks 'Very Difficult' \par China Says Has No Intention To 'Buy' Europe \par Obama Says Recovery 'Speeding Up' \par US Autos Sell At Record-High Prices \par Brazil Hackers Continue Wave Of Attacks \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN ASIA-PACIFIC EVENTS (times in GMT