\par\par \b \uc2 DJ WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Boosted By Strong U.S. Jobs Data\b0\par\par\uc2 By Javier E. David   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2012\par 23:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--A surprisingly strong jobs report boosted the dollar Friday, as evidence of a rebounding labor market helped diminish expectations of more expansionary monetary policy by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With markets still being roiled by Greece's efforts to restructure its debt in order to receive fresh financial assistance, investors cheered news that the U.S. economy added 243,000 jobs last month. The jobless rate fell to 8.3%, its lowest since February 2009.\par \par \uc2 Since last week's Fed policy meeting, in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledged to keep its key interest rate at near zero through late 2014, investors have been reluctant to hold dollars. Analysts said that a strengthening labor market may alleviate one of the Fed's major concerns about the economy, thus curtailing the likelihood of a new round of potential bond buying that tends to weaken the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The jobs report "forced traders to adjust their expectations for" a third round of quantitative easing, said Kathy Lien, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York. "We went from pricing in stimulus to pricing in a rate hike as early as 2014."\par \par \uc2 The strong data works at cross purposes. Although it helped staunch concerns amongst \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders about QE3, the numbers bolstered the market's appetite for stocks and higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That helped the euro reverse the day's losses despite ongoing jitters about Greece's ability to strike a long-awaited deal with its private bondholders.\par \par \uc2 Going forward, strong data "will be more positive for the euro than the dollar," Lien said, because euro-zone yields are currently higher than the rock-bottom returns offered in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday, the euro was at $1.3163 from $1.3144 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.64 from Y76.21, while the euro was at Y100.83 from Y100.20. The U.K. pound was at $1.5822 from $1.5807. The dollar was at CHF0.9190 from CHF0.9168, while the euro was at CHF1.2080 from CHF1.2050.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 78.939 from about 78.991.\par \par \uc2 One of the day's biggest movers was the yen, which weakened broadly on concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. Over the last several days, the dollar has steadily closed in on last year's record low at Y75.31, prompting Japanese officials to warn that they would take "decisive measures" to counteract the safe-haven buying that normally drives the yen higher.\par \par \uc2 Europe's debt crisis, however, remains a significant near-term risk. Greece's private creditors have yet to agree to a voluntary deal that averts a potentially messy default. The process has put renewed pressure on Portugal, whose government bond yields have skyrocketed amid fears that it could be the next euro-zone nation to endure a restructuring.\par \par \uc2 The fact that investors still maintain huge bets the euro will fall augurs for more gains, analysts say. If data and trends in Europe become more encouraging, traders will likely be prompted to cover short positions.\par \par \uc2 "You don't have to be bullish on the euro, but you have to be less bearish," said Andrew Wilkinson, chief economic strategist at Miller Tabak & Co. LLC. A resurgent euro could rally as high as $1.34 by the end of this month, he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Javier E. David, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4564; javier.david@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120203e82300akl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
