\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2012\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: ASIA MANUFACTURING PICTURE IMPROVING BUT STILL STRAINED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's manufacturing sector showed signs of improving health in January, but manufacturing and trade data elsewhere in Asia pointed to continuing weakness as slowing demand from Western economies takes its toll. (published 0507 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  JAN MANUFACTURING PMI RISES TO 57.5; FASTEST IN 8 MONTHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's manufacturing activity picked up sharply in January--the fastest pace in eight months--helped by new order growth, a survey showed Wednesday. (published 0521 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: S KOREA POSTS FIRST TRADE DEFICIT IN 2 YEARS AS GROWTH MOMENTUM STALLS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea ran its first trade deficit in two years in January while inflation eased to a one-year low, underscoring slowing economic momentum for the export-dependent country. (published 0242 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: INDIA DECEMBER TRADE DEFICIT $12.7 BLN VS $8.0 BLN YEAR AGO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's trade deficit in December widened to $12.7 billion from $8.0 billion a year earlier as export growth slowed due to falling global demand. (published 0523 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIA'S PM EXPECTS A$ TO STAY HIGH FOR YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar will likely stay high for years to come as investors use it to bet on China's growth and as record foreign investment flows into the nation's bonds, Prime Minister Julia Gillard said Wednesday. (published 0346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HK FINANCIAL SECRETARY: EXPECT 2012 GDP GROWTH AT 1%-3% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang said Wednesday he expects the city's economy to grow between 1% and 3% in 2012, slowing from last year due to the deteriorating global economy. (published 0326 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HK FINANCIAL SECRETARY: SEE 2012 HEADLINE CPI +3.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang said Wednesday that the government estimates the city's headline inflation for 2012 to be 3.5%, and added he expects inflation to ease significantly in the second half. (published 0344 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: INDONESIA INFLATION EASES IN JANUARY, BUT NO RATE CUT SEEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in Indonesia continued to ease in January, but most economists say that won't be enough to prompt the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut interest rates as inflationary pressures are likely to increase in coming months. (published 0512 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: THAI JANUARY INFLATION EASED; RATE OUTLOOK SEEN UNCHANGED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's consumer price index rose 3.38% in January from a year earlier, government data showed Wednesday, more slowly than December's 3.53% increase and economists' forecasts, reinforcing expectations that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would keep its policy interest rate unchanged at its next meeting. (published 0508 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN'S DOMESTIC AUTO SALES +40.7% ON YEAR IN JAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's domestic sales of new cars, trucks and buses soared 40.7% from a year earlier in January, rising for the fifth consecutive month, as the supply of vehicles increased as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Japanese car makers' factories returned to normal after the March 11 disaster. (published 0530 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA JAN PERFORMANCE OF MANUFACTURING INDEX +1.4 PTS TO 51.6 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's manufacturing sector grew for the second consecutive month in January but factory output remains constrained by the uncertain global outlook and strong offshore competition, a performance gauge produced by an industry group published Wednesday shows. (published 2230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA ON FEB 7 TO CONFIRM IF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SHIFTED TO "STEALTH" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will on Feb. 7 release detailed data on its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in the fourth quarter of 2011, a ministry official said Wednesday, figures that will make clear whether Tokyo was covertly active in the market after its highly publicized yen-selling campaign on Oct. 31. (published 0139 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA AVERAGE HOUSING PRICE LOWER IN JANUARY VS DECEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The average housing price for 100 major cities across China was lower for a fifth consecutive month in January, a survey showed Wednesday as the residential property sector remained cool, though the decline in property prices is losing steam. (published 0347 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MEXICO INFLATION COULD BE ABOVE 4% SOME MONTHS - CENTRAL BANKER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexican inflation could move above 4%, the top end of the Bank of Mexico's forecast, during some months this year, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Deputy Gov. Manuel Sanchez said Tuesday. (published 0359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AMERICA MOVIL BOND TO SELL 3-YEAR DIM SUM BOND - TERM SHEET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexico's \f28 \uc2 \f2 America Movil S.A.B. de C.V\f28 \uc2 \f2 . plans to sell an offshore yuan-denominated bond with a maturity of three years, according to a term sheet seen by Dow Jones Newswires Wednesday. (published 0218 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HITACHI CAPITAL CORP\f28 \uc2 \f2  MULLS SALE OF YUAN-DENOMINATED BOND - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hitachi Capital Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . is considering issuing offshore yuan-denominated bonds, a person familiar with the situation said Wednesday. (published 0512 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par FOCUS: JAPAN HOPES CHINA BOND BUYING WILL SPEED UP MARKET REFORMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda and his Chinese counterpart Wen Jiabao met in December, a deal by Tokyo to buy Chinese government bonds was hailed by the two as way to bring the major trading partners even closer together. (published 0237 GMT) [ 01-02-12 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120201e821000bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
