\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2012\par 05:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: CHINA PMIS RISE SLIGHTLY IN JANUARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two sets of China Purchasing Managers Index released Wednesday rose in January--a mildly positive signal for the world's second-largest economy. (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: S KOREA POSTS FIRST TRADE DEFICIT IN 2 YEARS AS GROWTH MOMENTUM STALLS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea ran its first trade deficit in two years in January while inflation eased to a one-year low, underscoring slowing economic momentum for the export-dependent country. (published 0242 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIA'S PM EXPECTS A$ TO STAY HIGH FOR YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar will likely stay high for years to come as investors use it to bet on China's growth and as record foreign investment flows into the nation's bonds, Prime Minister Julia Gillard said Wednesday. (published 0346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HK FINANCIAL SECRETARY: EXPECT 2012 GDP GROWTH AT 1%-3% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang said Wednesday he expects the city's economy to grow between 1% and 3% in 2012, slowing from last year due to the deteriorating global economy. (published 0326 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HK FINANCIAL SECRETARY: SEE 2012 HEADLINE CPI +3.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang said Wednesday that the government estimates the city's headline inflation for 2012 to be 3.5%, and added he expects inflation to ease significantly in the second half. (published 0344 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: THAI JANUARY CPI UP 3.38% ON YEAR; 3.4% RISE EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's consumer price index rose 3.38% in January from a year earlier, slowing from December's 3.53% increase, Commerce Ministry Permanent Secretary Yanyong Phuangrach said Wednesday. (published 0342 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA JAN PERFORMANCE OF MANUFACTURING INDEX +1.4 PTS TO 51.6 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's manufacturing sector grew for the second consecutive month in January but factory output remains constrained by the uncertain global outlook and strong offshore competition, a performance gauge produced by an industry group published Wednesday shows. (published 2230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA ON FEB 7 TO CONFIRM IF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SHIFTED TO "STEALTH" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will on Feb. 7 release detailed data on its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in the fourth quarter of 2011, a ministry official said Wednesday, figures that will make clear whether Tokyo was covertly active in the market after its highly publicized yen-selling campaign on Oct. 31. (published 0139 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA AVERAGE HOUSING PRICE LOWER IN JANUARY VS DECEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The average housing price for 100 major cities across China was lower for a fifth consecutive month in January, a survey showed Wednesday as the residential property sector remained cool, though the decline in property prices is losing steam. (published 0347 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MEXICO INFLATION COULD BE ABOVE 4% SOME MONTHS - CENTRAL BANKER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexican inflation could move above 4%, the top end of the Bank of Mexico's forecast, during some months this year, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Deputy Gov. Manuel Sanchez said Tuesday. (published 0359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DAH SING BANK SELLS S$225 MLN OF BONDS AT 4.875% YIELD - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dah Sing Banking Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . has sold S$225 million worth of subordinated bonds, a person familiar with the matter said Wednesday. (published 0049 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AMERICA MOVIL BOND TO SELL 3-YEAR DIM SUM BOND - TERM SHEET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexico's \f28 \uc2 \f2 America Movil S.A.B. de C.V\f28 \uc2 \f2 . plans to sell an offshore yuan-denominated bond with a maturity of three years, according to a term sheet seen by Dow Jones Newswires Wednesday. (published 0218 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par FOCUS: JAPAN HOPES CHINA BOND BUYING WILL SPEED UP MARKET REFORMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda and his Chinese counterpart Wen Jiabao met in December, a deal by Tokyo to buy Chinese government bonds was hailed by the two as way to bring the major trading partners even closer together. (published 0237 GMT) [ 01-02-12 0402GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120201e8210007a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
